###############################################################################
#  jovi-mall — environment template
#
#  Regenerated from source. Every variable `src/` reads is listed here exactly
#  once, grouped by subsystem, with where the value comes from and what changes
#  in the service when you set it.
#
#  ── How to read this file ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#    an uncommented line    you are expected to set this. The value shown is a
#                           working local default, not a production one.
#    a commented-out line   optional. The value shown is the compiled-in default
#                           that applies while the line stays commented out.
#                           Uncomment only to change it.
#
#  ── Two things that are not obvious ─────────────────────────────────────────
#  1. `src/config/env.ts` validates this environment at boot and refuses to
#     start on a problem it can prove (a provider named without its
#     credentials, an integer that will not parse, a variable whose name is no
#     longer read). It prints EVERY problem at once. Warnings print and the
#     service continues.
#  2. Almost every optional variable is read by a module config that silently
#     substitutes its default for a value it cannot parse. That is why the
#     validator exists, and why `COD_DEPOSIT_DEADLINE_DAYS=two` is an error
#     here rather than a shrug.
#
#  ── Where a secret COMES FROM (plan step 2.E.1) ─────────────────────────────
#  Every secret-bearing block carries a SOURCE line naming the ORIGIN of the
#  value. Three kinds, and the difference decides who can rotate it:
#
#    SOURCE: generated · openssl rand -hex 32
#        we mint it. Rotating it is ours to do, on our schedule.
#    SOURCE: <provider> console → <where>
#        they issue it. Rotating it means going there; we only carry the value.
#    SOURCE: SHARED with <service> as <THEIR_VAR_NAME>
#        one value, two services, and usually TWO DIFFERENT NAMES. Rotating one
#        side alone breaks the seam SILENTLY. There are four of these; all four
#        are listed in ../docs/RUNBOOK.md § Rotation.
#
#  At deploy time the value itself comes from the HOST'S OWN SECRET STORE and
#  never from a file in this repository (ADR-019 D-1). This template is the
#  variable → source MAPPING; it is not, and must never become, the vault.
#
#  Rotation procedure, for all of the above: ../docs/RUNBOOK.md § Rotation.
#
#  NOTHING IN THIS FILE IS A REAL CREDENTIAL. Every secret is a placeholder.
###############################################################################


###############################################################################
#  1. CORE RUNTIME
###############################################################################

# development | test | production
# Production turns on: secure cookies, secret-strength enforcement, log
# persistence to Mongo, HTTP access logging, and the /metrics token gate.
NODE_ENV=development

# TCP port the HTTP server binds.
PORT=8022

# MongoDB connection string. Get it from your Mongo deployment (Atlas: Connect →
# Drivers). Transactions are used throughout, so this MUST be a replica set —
# a standalone mongod fails every checkout, every status transition and every
# COD settlement at runtime, not at boot. Local dev: `rs.initiate()` once.
#
# NOTE the name: `server.ts` reads MONGO_URI. `MONGODB_URI` is read only by
# `src/scripts/**`; setting that one alone boots cleanly against localhost.
#
# SOURCE: Mongo deployment (Atlas → Connect → Drivers), or the compose stack.
#         Treat as a SECRET in production — the connection string carries the
#         password inline, which is why no *_URI is ever served by
#         GET /system/config (see exposed-config.ts).
MONGO_URI=mongodb://localhost:27017/jovi_mall

# Redis connection string. Connects LAZILY — never at boot — so an unreachable
# Redis does not stop startup and does not fail readiness by default. It backs
# email tokens, WhatsApp codes, booking slot holds, download tokens and the rate
# limiter, worker locks, connection codes and passwordless sign-in sessions, on
# dedicated DB indices 3–14 — 4 and 9 are RETIRED, not free (see
# `infra/redis/redis.factory.ts`, which carries the authoritative catalogue).
# With Redis down: booking holds fall back to the transactional CAS, and the
# rate limiter FAILS OPEN rather than 500-ing every request.
#
# SOURCE: Redis deployment, or the compose stack. Treat as a SECRET in
#         production for the same reason as MONGO_URI — `redis://user:pass@host`
#         carries the credential inline.
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379


###############################################################################
#  2. HTTP INTAKE  (`src/config/http.config.ts`)
###############################################################################

# Whether Express may believe X-Forwarded-For when resolving `req.ip`.
# Accepts Express's own vocabulary: false | true | a hop count | a subnet.
#
# There is no safe default, which is why it is off unless set:
#   unset behind a proxy  → every request reports the proxy's address, so the
#                           anonymous rate-limit bucket is one bucket for the
#                           whole internet and the audit trail records the proxy
#                           as the actor for every request.
#   `true` with nothing stripping the header → any caller spoofs `req.ip` per
#                           request and is never rate limited.
# A hop count is usually right: it says how many proxies you actually run.
# TRUST_PROXY=false

# Origins a BROWSER may make credentialed cross-origin requests from.
# Comma-separated, EXACT match — no wildcards, no suffix matching.
#
# Empty (the default) refuses every cross-origin browser client. It fails
# closed, which is correct, but the symptom is a CORS error in the browser and a
# 200 in the server log — it looks like a frontend bug. Set it to your
# dashboard and storefront origins, scheme and port included, no trailing slash.
#
# Recognised schemes are http, https and `capacitor` — anything else refuses the
# boot. A dashboard wrapped in Capacitor runs on `capacitor://localhost` (iOS) or
# `https://localhost` (Android), and both are in the value below deliberately.
# They were absent from this template while the running `.env` carried them by
# hand — and this file IS the deployment contract, so every environment built
# from it CORS-refused the WebView client the whole `/api/auth/mobile/*`
# namespace exists for. Keep them in step with geo-tracker's own
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS, which gates the tracking WebSocket that same client opens.
#
# ⚠ `https://localhost` grants credentialed CORS to any HTTPS server on the
# user's own loopback. Narrow, but real — DROP IT from a production allowlist
# unless a native Android build actually needs it. (Capacitor can be configured
# to a custom hostname if you would rather not.) `capacitor://` carries no such
# exposure: no ordinary server can claim that scheme.
#
# ⚠ BOTH DASHBOARDS TOOK THAT PARENTHETICAL, AND THE TEMPLATE DID NOT FOLLOW THEM
# (added 2026-09-08). `vendor-dash` and `agency-dash` set a custom Capacitor
# `server.hostname` — `vendor.wi-mall.internal` / `agency.wi-mall.internal`, on
# `.internal` precisely because it can never route publicly, so the WebView's
# request interception can never shadow a real domain. Each presents THREE origins,
# one per platform-and-scheme:
#
#   Android release  → https://<app>.wi-mall.internal
#   Android LAN dev  → http://<app>.wi-mall.internal    (androidScheme flips on CAP_LAN_DEV=1)
#   iOS              → capacitor://<app>.wi-mall.internal
#
# All six went into the running `.env` by hand and into no template, so this file —
# which IS the deployment contract — again described a CORS posture no environment
# actually ran. That is the second occurrence of the defect the paragraph above
# records; the first was `capacitor://localhost` itself.
#
# `landing` needs nothing extra: it sets no custom hostname, so its origins are the
# plain `https://localhost` / `capacitor://localhost` already listed.
#
# ⚠ geo-tracker's own ALLOWED_ORIGINS carries only AGENCY's three, not vendor's,
# and that asymmetry is deliberate — `vendor-dash` opens no tracking socket. Do not
# "reconcile" the two lists by copying this one across.
ALLOWED_ORIGINS='http://localhost:5173,http://localhost:5174,http://localhost:3000,http://localhost:5175,https://localhost,capacitor://localhost,https://agency.wi-mall.internal,http://agency.wi-mall.internal,capacitor://agency.wi-mall.internal,https://vendor.wi-mall.internal,http://vendor.wi-mall.internal,capacitor://vendor.wi-mall.internal'

# Ceiling on a parsed JSON / urlencoded body. Exceeding it is a clean
# REQUEST_BODY_TOO_LARGE; before this had a name it surfaced as a 500.
# JSON_BODY_LIMIT=1mb

# Ceiling on ONE path — POST /api/internal/bot/files/inbound, which carries a
# photo a customer sent in a chat as base64. Base64 inflates by a third, so the
# 1mb global ceiling refuses every real image; app.ts mounts a wider parser on
# that path alone rather than raising the budget every other route must defend.
# This is only the backstop — the refusal a customer actually reads is the 8 MB
# DECODED limit in bot-file.controller.ts, which is lower so it always fires
# first. Raise both together or the parser starts producing a bare 413 with no
# error code for the chat to relay.
# BOT_FILE_BODY_LIMIT=12mb

# Ceiling on a raw gateway webhook body — deliberately larger. A Stripe event
# carrying a fully expanded object is legitimate traffic, and a 413 here does
# not inconvenience a caller, it loses a payment notification.
# WEBHOOK_BODY_LIMIT=2mb


###############################################################################
#  3. AUTHENTICATION  (`src/config/secrets.config.ts`, `cookie.config.ts`)
###############################################################################

# Signs and verifies HS256 access tokens. Generate with:
#     openssl rand -hex 32
#
# SHARED VERBATIM WITH geo-tracker — it must be byte-identical to geo-tracker's
# JWT_SECRET or every forwarded viewer token fails there. Boot fails if unset;
# in production it also fails if under 16 chars or a known placeholder.
#
# SOURCE: generated · openssl rand -hex 32
# SOURCE: SHARED with geo-tracker as JWT_SECRET  ⚠ LOCKSTEP ROTATION
#         ⚠ And the two sides do NOT fail the same way. This service refuses to
#         boot without it (`assertSigningSecrets()`); geo-tracker's
#         `getEnv("JWT_SECRET", "secret")` falls back to the literal string
#         "secret" and boots happily, accepting anything signed with it. So on
#         a half-done rotation the loud failure is always on THIS side, and the
#         dangerous one is always on theirs. ../docs/RUNBOOK.md § Rotation.
JWT_SECRET=replace_me_openssl_rand_hex_32

# Signs refresh tokens. Falls back to JWT_SECRET when unset — a real secret
# either way, but using one secret for both means a stolen access token can be
# replayed as a refresh token. Generate a SECOND, different value.
#
# SOURCE: generated · openssl rand -hex 32 (a SECOND, different value)
# JWT_REFRESH_SECRET=replace_me_a_different_openssl_rand_hex_32

# Access / refresh token lifetimes, in SECONDS. These also drive the cookie
# max-age, so the cookie and the token cannot disagree.
# Access tokens are stateless: shortening this is what bounds how long a
# suspended account keeps working before `requireAuth` re-checks it.
# AUTH_ACCESS_TOKEN_TTL=900        # 15 minutes
# AUTH_REFRESH_TOKEN_TTL=2592000   # 30 days

# The ABSOLUTE ceiling on one sign-in, in SECONDS — the longest a session may
# last however much it is used. Distinct from the refresh TTL above, which bounds
# how long a token may go UNUSED: every re-issue mints a fresh pair at full
# lifetime, so without this the 30-day window slides forever and a stolen refresh
# token an attacker keeps using never lapses (A-3).
#
# Carried statelessly, as an `auth_time` claim stamped at login and copied
# unchanged through every re-issue. Past the cap, both credential paths answer
# `401 AUTH_SESSION_CAP_REACHED` — which is NOT refreshable: a client must route
# to login, never retry. Lowering this signs people out more often and is a
# visible event for every native client, because a bearer client has no silent
# renewal. Raising it above ~180 days makes the ceiling mostly theatre.
#
# SOURCE: policy · ADR-A03 D-1 (jovi-mall/docs/ADR-A03-SESSION-CAP.md)
# AUTH_ABSOLUTE_SESSION_CAP=7776000  # 90 days

# Cookie `Domain` attribute, applied in production only. Set it to the parent
# domain (leading dot) when the API and the dashboards are on sibling subdomains
# — otherwise the browser scopes the auth cookie to the API host and the
# dashboard silently never sends it.
# AUTH_COOKIE_DOMAIN=.yourdomain.com

# Signs the OAuth `state` parameter (CSRF protection on the Google Calendar
# connect flow). Falls back to JWT_SECRET when unset, which works; a separate
# value keeps a short-lived browser-visible token off your session-signing key.
# Generate with `openssl rand -hex 32`.
#
# SOURCE: generated · openssl rand -hex 32
# OAUTH_STATE_SECRET=

# Public base URL of THIS API, used to build links in outbound email
# (verification, password reset). Defaults to http://localhost:$PORT, which
# produces unclickable links in any deployment a user reaches from elsewhere.
# API_PUBLIC_URL=https://api.yourdomain.com

# WhatsApp number a user is told to message to CONNECT their WhatsApp account
# (they send `/connect` to it and the bot replies with a code). Digits only, no
# `+`. Read by GET /api/me/connections to build the instruction and the wa.me
# deep link. Blank disables the instruction, not the flow — a user who already
# knows the number can still connect.
#
# This used to say "to sign in by WhatsApp". There has never been a WhatsApp
# sign-in on this service; the variable has only ever addressed the linking bot.
# WA_BOT_NUMBER=

# ── Self-service contact change (`modules/users/config/contact-change.config.ts`) ──
#
# How long a change to the email or phone an account SIGNS IN WITH stays open.
# Both are deliberately shorter than the 24h registration verification window:
# that token proves an address somebody just typed into a signup form, while
# these bound the period in which a confirmation landing in the wrong mailbox —
# or a WhatsApp number connected for an unrelated reason — would still move
# somebody's login identifier.
#
# The identifier itself never moves until the change is confirmed, in either
# case, so an expired request costs nothing but a retry.

# Lifetime of the emailed confirmation link. Also the exposure window of a token
# the platform has already handed to a mail provider.
# CONTACT_CHANGE_EMAIL_TTL_SECONDS=3600

# How long a requested PHONE change waits for its proof. Longer, because the
# proof is not delivered — the account holder has to connect that number on
# WhatsApp (`POST /api/me/connections`), possibly on a handset that is not to
# hand. There is no SMS OTP here and no outbound WhatsApp code: a message to a
# number outside the 24h service window needs an approved paid template billed
# to a credit wallet, and a customer has none.
# CONTACT_CHANGE_PHONE_TTL_SECONDS=86400


###############################################################################
#  4. RATE LIMITING  (`src/api/rate-limit/policy.ts`)
#
#  Three layers: an IP-scoped one before auth, an identity-scoped one at the
#  tail of `requireAuth`, and an IP-scoped one on /api/public/*. Requests per
#  60s window.
#
#  Every ceiling here is a BACKSTOP, not a budget — set so no real user of any
#  role can reach it and a runaway loop or scraper can. The exception is
#  RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_PER_MIN, which is a security control.
#
#  The store FAILS OPEN: with Redis down, requests pass rather than 500.
#  /api/health*, /metrics and /api/webhooks/* are exempt (see ADR-016).
###############################################################################

# Layer A — IP-scoped, before auth. Sits ABOVE every identity ceiling on
# purpose: it should only fire on a genuine flood from one address.
# RATE_LIMIT_GLOBAL_PER_MIN=1200

# Layer B — identity-scoped, per signed-in user. The ordering is about request
# density per role, not trust: agent is a mobile client polling offers, admin
# loads multi-panel dashboards, customer browses.
# RATE_LIMIT_ADMIN_PER_MIN=1200
# RATE_LIMIT_AGENT_PER_MIN=1200
# RATE_LIMIT_VENDOR_PER_MIN=900
# RATE_LIMIT_AGENCY_PER_MIN=900
# RATE_LIMIT_CUSTOMER_PER_MIN=600
# RATE_LIMIT_ANON_PER_MIN=600

# The credential bucket, per IP — the paths that PRESENT a credential: login,
# register, forgot/reset-password, browser+mobile login, add-role. The one strict
# number here. It bounds one source spraying a common password across many
# accounts, which per-account lockout cannot see. It is also the DEFAULT for any
# new route under /api/auth, so a credential endpoint added later inherits it.
# Prefer moving a path to the session bucket below over raising this.
# RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_PER_MIN=20

# The session bucket, per IP — the /api/auth paths that EXTEND a session rather
# than open one: both refresh routes, /auth/me, both auth-me routes. They present
# no password, so the spraying argument above does not apply to them; sharing one
# counter meant a user whose refresh was refused got signed out and then found the
# sign-in refused too, by their neighbours' traffic behind the same NAT.
#
# 300 is set from /auth/me, not from refresh: refresh at ~4/hour/user saturates it
# at ~1200 users on one address, but a dashboard polling /auth/me once a minute
# would saturate 120 at 120 concurrent users, which is reachable. Both auth-me and
# me run behind requireAuth, so Layer B already bounds each person (600-1200) and
# Layer A still bounds the address (1200) on top — this counter's remaining job is
# only to stop one client looping on refresh.
# RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_SESSION_PER_MIN=300

# The storefront bucket, per IP — /api/public/* only (catalog, stores, plans,
# articles). It exists because anonymous browse traffic otherwise shares Layer
# A's single counter with everything else on the address: a product grid fires
# a list call plus a categories call per page view, and behind an office NAT or
# a mobile carrier that is hundreds of people on one bucket — so the storefront
# is what trips it, for everybody, including the signed-in shoppers on the same
# address who then cannot check out.
#
# Set ABOVE the global ceiling on purpose. These are cacheable, unauthenticated
# reads of already-published data carrying `Cache-Control: public, max-age=300`
# — the cheapest requests the service serves. Layer A still applies on top
# (/api/public is deliberately NOT exempt), so the effective ceiling is the
# lower of the two and this is a widening, not a tightening.
# RATE_LIMIT_PUBLIC_PER_MIN=3000

# Layer C — the connection-code bucket, on POST /api/me/connections alone. Per
# IP, per minute. The first per-endpoint limiter in the service, and it is one
# because that endpoint takes a GUESSABLE secret: a 6-character code is 2^30,
# a large number for a person and a small one for a loop.
#
# The tighter control is the per-account attempt counter (5 per 10 minutes, in
# connection-code.store.ts). This exists alongside it because that one keys on
# the ACCOUNT, and accounts are free — registering a hundred buys a hundred
# fresh counters. Keying on the address is what makes that expensive.
#
# Deliberately IP-scoped even though the route runs behind requireAuth: an
# identity scope here would be the counter the attacker already controls.
# RATE_LIMIT_CONNECTION_CODE_PER_MIN=30


###############################################################################
#  5. SERVICE-TO-SERVICE TOKENS
#
#  Two separate secrets, deliberately. geo-tracker may ask whether an agent can
#  be tracked; wi-admin may confirm a remittance and move money. One shared
#  value would make either compromise the other's. Both FAIL CLOSED when unset.
###############################################################################

# Authenticates geo-tracker on inbound calls (`/api/internal/agents/*`,
# `/api/tracking/*`). Must equal geo-tracker's NODE_API_SERVICE_TOKEN.
# Unset disables that API entirely — geo-tracker then cannot resolve "may this
# viewer track this agent", so every live subscription fails authorization.
#
# SOURCE: generated · openssl rand -hex 32
# SOURCE: SHARED with geo-tracker as NODE_API_SERVICE_TOKEN  ⚠ LOCKSTEP,
#         AND THE TWO NAMES DIFFER. Confirmed in source: geo-tracker sends it
#         as `Authorization: Bearer` (platform/nodeclient/client.go) and
#         `internal-agent.routes.ts` verifies it. Rotating one side alone gives
#         a 401 on every geo→jovi permission query.
INTERNAL_SERVICE_TOKEN=must_equal_jovi_mall_INTERNAL_SERVICE_TOKEN_trust_as_is

# Authenticates the wi-admin service on `/api/internal/admin/*`. Must equal
# wi-admin's JOVI_MALL_SERVICE_TOKEN. Unset disables the internal admin API —
# the intended local default until the admin service is running.
#
# This is a FULL-PRIVILEGE credential: authorization is resolved in wi-admin and
# this service re-checks nothing. In production a placeholder or a value under
# 16 characters refuses the boot, because an API that is open and looks
#
# SOURCE: generated · openssl rand -hex 32
# SOURCE: SHARED with wi-admin as JOVI_MALL_SERVICE_TOKEN  ⚠ LOCKSTEP,
#         AND THE TWO NAMES DIFFER. wi-admin sends it as `X-Service-Token`
#         (`infra/platform/platform.client.ts`), not as a bearer.
#         Deliberately NOT the same value as INTERNAL_SERVICE_TOKEN above.
INTERNAL_ADMIN_SERVICE_TOKEN=must_equal_jovi_mall_INTERNAL_ADMIN_SERVICE_TOKEN_trust_as_is


###############################################################################
#  6. FRONTEND URLs
#
#  Used to build deep links in notifications. When one is unset, that audience's
#  notifications are delivered WITHOUT their action button — in-app, email, push
#  and WhatsApp alike. The message still arrives; it just cannot be acted on,
#  which reads as a broken template rather than a missing variable.
###############################################################################

VENDOR_APP_URL=http://localhost:5173
AGENCY_APP_URL=http://localhost:5174
AGENT_APP_URL=http://localhost:5175

# Customer-facing storefront. Also the base for customer notification links, and for EVERY
# emailed-token PAGE: the password-reset link, the magic sign-in link, the email-change
# confirmation and the registration-verification link. All four point at a page rather than
# at this API, because a GET that mutates is spent by whatever prefetches the mail. Each
# falls back to API_PUBLIC_URL when this is unset, so a local box still works.
# ONE base for all four — a per-role base would mean a per-role page; the requesting role
# travels in the link as `app=` instead. Never a caller-supplied URL: these pages are
# reachable with no session.
STOREFRONT_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Base for a vendor's public store page: `<base>/<store-slug>`.
# Must be `<STOREFRONT_URL>/shop/stores` — that is the route the storefront serves.
# STORE_PUBLIC_URL_BASE=https://yourdomain.com/shop/stores


###############################################################################
#  7. FILE STORAGE  (`src/core/storage/`)
#
#  ⚠ These names changed. If you are migrating from an older template, the old
#  spellings (CLOUDINARY_*, FIREBASE_*, LOCAL_STORAGE_*) are read by NOTHING —
#  uploads silently went to local disk. The boot validator now refuses to start
#  when it finds one, rather than letting that repeat.
###############################################################################

# local | firebase | cloudinary | r2
# `local` writes to the container filesystem, which is lost on every restart and
# redeploy — fine for development, a data-loss bug in production.
#
# SOURCE: SHARED with wi-admin as STORAGE_PROVIDER, STORAGE_LOCAL_URL,
#         STORAGE_FIREBASE_BUCKET and STORAGE_FIREBASE_PUBLIC — **the SAME NAMES
#         on both sides**, so this pair can be diffed directly rather than
#         translated. Added 2026-08-25 by BR-015 / wi-admin ADR-021 D-3: its media
#         library builds FileDetail.url itself instead of taking a
#         POST /files/resolve hop per page, so it needs the provider and the base
#         URL. It holds no bucket, no credential and no signing key — it computes
#         a URL for a key it has already read, and nothing more.
#
#         ⚠ LOCKSTEP, and a MISMATCH IS SILENT. Nothing compares the two values:
#         a divergence fails no boot on either side and logs nothing. It produces
#         URLs that 404 on wi-admin's media library, which reads as "the files are
#         gone". This is the SIXTH cross-service shared value on the platform.
#         backend/docs/RUNBOOK.md § Rotation.
#
#         Note wi-admin reproduces `local`, `firebase` and `r2` — the r2 arm needs a
#         FIFTH shared name, STORAGE_R2_PUBLIC_URL, spelled the same way there.
#         Under `cloudinary` its media library reports every url as null (and says
#         so, via meta.publicUrlsConfigured) rather than guessing at the SDK's URL
#         builder.
#         STORAGE_LOCAL_PATH is NOT shared — it is a filesystem path only this
#         service uses.
STORAGE_PROVIDER=local

# ── local ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# STORAGE_LOCAL_PATH=./storage
# ⚠ STORAGE_LOCAL_URL is SHARED with wi-admin under the same name — see above.
# STORAGE_LOCAL_URL=http://localhost:8022/api/files

# ── firebase (required when STORAGE_PROVIDER=firebase) ───────────────────────
#
# SOURCE: Google Cloud console → IAM & Admin → Service Accounts → Keys → JSON.
#         All four values come out of that one downloaded key file. The private
#         key rotates by issuing a NEW key and deleting the old one there; we
#         only carry it.
#
# From the Firebase console → Project settings → Service accounts → Generate new
# private key. This is the STORAGE project; FCM below uses a separate one.
# Keep the private key on one line with literal \n escapes — they are unescaped
# on read.
# STORAGE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
# STORAGE_FIREBASE_CLIENT_EMAIL=
# STORAGE_FIREBASE_PRIVATE_KEY="-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\n...\n-----END PRIVATE KEY-----\n"
# STORAGE_FIREBASE_BUCKET=your-project.appspot.com
# Whether objects are world-readable by URL. `false` means every read needs a
# signed URL.
# STORAGE_FIREBASE_PUBLIC=true

# ── cloudinary (required when STORAGE_PROVIDER=cloudinary) ───────────────────
#
# SOURCE: Cloudinary console → Settings → Access Keys.
#         ⚠ Note the STORAGE_ prefix. `CLOUDINARY_API_KEY` (no prefix) is read
#         by NOTHING here — see RENAMED_VARS in config/env.ts. Setting the
#         unprefixed name configures object storage that never activates, and
#         every upload silently goes to a container disk instead.
#
# From the Cloudinary dashboard → Product Environment Credentials.
# STORAGE_CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME=
# STORAGE_CLOUDINARY_API_KEY=
# STORAGE_CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET=
# Folder every asset is filed under, so one account can host several
# environments without collisions.
# STORAGE_CLOUDINARY_FOLDER_PREFIX=jovi

# ── r2 · Cloudflare R2 (required when STORAGE_PROVIDER=r2) ───────────────────
#
# SOURCE: Cloudflare dashboard → R2 → Manage R2 API Tokens → Create API Token,
#         permission "Object Read & Write", scoped to BOTH buckets below. The
#         token page shows the Access Key ID and the Secret Access Key ONCE — the
#         secret cannot be re-read, and rotation is issue-a-new-token → deploy →
#         delete-the-old-one. The Account ID is on the R2 overview page and is the
#         ONLY thing needed to derive the S3 endpoint
#         (https://<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com) — there is no endpoint
#         variable here and there must not be one.
#
# ⚠ TWO BUCKETS, AND THAT IS THE SECURITY MECHANISM (ADR-A01 D-2).
#   R2 HAS NO PER-OBJECT ACL. `ACL: 'public-read'` is accepted and silently
#   discarded; an object is reachable if and only if its BUCKET carries a public
#   binding (a custom domain, or the r2.dev development URL). So the three private
#   trees — digital/, shipments/, ticket-attachments/ — can only be kept off the
#   CDN by living in a bucket that has no public binding at all. The provider
#   routes every put/get/delete on `isPrivateStorageKey(key)`.
#
#   Setting these two to the SAME bucket REFUSES THE BOOT, in config/env.ts and
#   again in the provider constructor. It would put every paid digital product and
#   every delivery-proof photograph — a real address and a timestamp — on a public
#   CDN, permanently, fetchable by anyone who saw the key once.
#
#   Before deploying, confirm in the dashboard that the PRIVATE bucket has NO
#   custom domain and that its r2.dev public development URL is DISABLED. Nothing
#   in this codebase can check that for you.
# STORAGE_R2_ACCOUNT_ID=
# STORAGE_R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# STORAGE_R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# The PUBLIC bucket. Carries the Cloudflare custom domain named below.
# STORAGE_R2_BUCKET=wi-mall-public
# The PRIVATE bucket. NO custom domain, r2.dev DISABLED. Must differ from above.
# STORAGE_R2_PRIVATE_BUCKET=wi-mall-private
# ⚠ SHARED with wi-admin under the SAME NAME, and a MISMATCH IS SILENT exactly
#   like STORAGE_LOCAL_URL above: no boot failure, no log line, just 404s on a
#   screen full of thumbnails. ../docs/RUNBOOK.md § Rotation.
#
#   This is the CUSTOM DOMAIN bound to the public bucket — NOT the S3 API endpoint
#   and NOT an r2.dev URL. NO TRAILING SLASH: it is concatenated as
#   `${base}/${key}`, so a trailing slash emits `//key`, which R2 treats as a
#   different key and 404s. The boot validator refuses one.
# STORAGE_R2_PUBLIC_URL=https://cdn.yourdomain.com

# Ceiling on a single digital-product asset, in BYTES. Applied by the upload
# middleware, so a larger file is rejected before it is buffered.
#
# ⚠ 150 MB, not the 500 MB this used to say, and the ceiling is the CONTAINER's
# not the storage provider's. `multer.memoryStorage()` buffers the whole file in
# process memory before any validator or provider sees a byte, and Node Buffers
# live OUTSIDE the V8 heap — so `--max-old-space-size` does not bound them, the
# container does. jovi-mall runs at `mem_limit: 768m` in docker-compose.prod.yml.
# A 500 MB upload OOM-killed the container, whichever provider was configured.
# Raise this only together with that limit.
# MAX_DIGITAL_ASSET_SIZE=157286400   # 150 MB


###############################################################################
#  8. UPLOAD POLICY  (`src/core/uploads/upload-config.ts`)
#
#  Note the polarity: most of these read `!== 'false'`, so they are ON unless
#  explicitly disabled.
###############################################################################

# UPLOAD_MAX_FILES_PER_REQUEST=10
# UPLOAD_MAX_TOTAL_SIZE_BYTES=52428800   # 50 MB across one multipart request

# Virus scanning. `blockOnFailure` decides what happens when the SCANNER itself
# fails (not when it finds something): true rejects the upload, false accepts it
# unscanned. Keep it true — a scanner that fails open is the configuration that
# produced this finding in the first place (S-2).
#
# PROVIDER is a closed set and every value that cannot scan REFUSES AT BOOT
# (`assertUploadScannerSafe`) rather than degrading quietly:
#   clamav — the real one, `clamd` over TCP. The only value valid in production.
#   mock   — a TEST DOUBLE that returns clean for every byte. Development only;
#            refused when NODE_ENV=production. It is the built-in DEFAULT, which
#            is exactly why forgetting this variable must not start a server.
#   cloud  — declared in the config type, never implemented. Always refused.
# UPLOAD_VIRUS_SCAN_ENABLED=true
# UPLOAD_VIRUS_SCAN_PROVIDER=clamav
# UPLOAD_VIRUS_SCAN_BLOCK_ON_FAILURE=true

# Where clamd is. In the workspace compose stack this is the `clamav` service;
# run natively and it is a local daemon on the standard port.
#
# ⚠ TIMEOUT: the whole scan — connect, stream, reply — not an idle timeout. It is
# what makes `blockOnFailure` reachable at all: a daemon that hangs never returns
# a verdict for it to act on, and the upload request stays open. 30 s is generous
# for a 25 MB stream on a local socket; raise it only alongside clamd's own
# StreamMaxLength.
#
# ⚠ StreamMaxLength (clamd's setting, not ours) bounds the file size clamd will
# accept — 25 MB by default, which is SMALLER than several of the per-MIME caps
# above. Over it, clamd answers ERROR, which surfaces as a scan failure and (with
# blockOnFailure) refuses the upload. Set it to match. See ../docs/RUNBOOK.md § 1.
#
# SOURCE: infrastructure · the `clamav` service in ../docker-compose.yml
# UPLOAD_CLAMAV_HOST=127.0.0.1
# UPLOAD_CLAMAV_PORT=3310
# UPLOAD_CLAMAV_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# Per-user storage quotas, enforced across every upload path.
# UPLOAD_USER_QUOTAS_ENABLED=true
# UPLOAD_USER_QUOTA_MAX_FILES=1000
# UPLOAD_USER_QUOTA_MAX_BYTES=5368709120   # 5 GB

# Content fingerprinting — the hash that makes duplicate detection possible.
# Turning it off disables duplicate detection regardless of the two below.
# UPLOAD_FINGERPRINTING_ENABLED=true
# UPLOAD_FINGERPRINT_ALGORITHM=sha256

# Duplicate handling. Detection only REPORTS a duplicate; BLOCK decides whether
# the second upload is refused. Note the different polarity of the two.
# UPLOAD_DUPLICATE_DETECTION_ENABLED=true
# UPLOAD_DUPLICATE_BLOCK=false

# UPLOAD_OBSERVABILITY_ENABLED=true
# UPLOAD_LOG_LEVEL=info


###############################################################################
#  9. FILE CLEANUP & STORAGE LIFECYCLE  (`src/config/file-cleanup.config.ts`)
#
#  Two stages: detach (media unlinked from an inactive product or a settled
#  ticket), then delete (a file with no live references for the grace period).
###############################################################################

# Master switch, and the safety catch. DRY_RUN defaults to TRUE: the sweep
# computes and audits what it WOULD remove and removes nothing. Flip it to
# false only once you have read a sweep's output and believe it.
# FILE_CLEANUP_ENABLED=true
# FILE_CLEANUP_DRY_RUN=true

# Daily sweep schedule (node-cron, server local time) and per-stage batch cap.
# FILE_CLEANUP_CRON=0 4 * * *
# FILE_CLEANUP_BATCH_SIZE=200

# Stage A — detach.
# Days of no paid order before a product's media is detached.
# FILE_CLEANUP_PRODUCT_DAYS=45
# Days after a ticket reaches a terminal status before its attachments detach.
# FILE_CLEANUP_TICKET_DAYS=15
# Comma-separated. Order payment_status values that count as activity and reset
# the inactivity clock.
# FILE_CLEANUP_ORDER_STATUSES=paid
# Comma-separated. Ticket statuses that count as terminal.
# FILE_CLEANUP_TICKET_STATUSES=resolved,closed

# Stage B — delete. Days a file may sit with zero live references before it is
# permanently removed from the storage provider.
# FILE_CLEANUP_LONELY_DAYS=15
# Never delete a file backing a live customer download entitlement. Turning this
# off can revoke access a customer has already paid for.
# FILE_CLEANUP_PROTECT_ENTITLEMENTS=true

# Per-stage kill switches.
# FILE_CLEANUP_STAGE_DETACH_ENABLED=true
# FILE_CLEANUP_STAGE_TICKET_ENABLED=true
# FILE_CLEANUP_STAGE_DELETE_ENABLED=true
# FILE_CLEANUP_STAGE_ALERT_ENABLED=true

# Storage-usage percentages at which a vendor is warned. Comma-separated,
# ascending, each 1–100.
# FILE_CLEANUP_ALERT_THRESHOLDS=80,90,100
# Fallback per-vendor cap in BYTES when no plan limit resolves.
# FILE_CLEANUP_DEFAULT_STORAGE_BYTES=5368709120   # 5 GB


###############################################################################
#  10. EMAIL  (`src/modules/mail/`)
###############################################################################

# console | smtp | brevo | resend | chain
#
# `console` prints the message and delivers nothing. In production that silently
# swallows verification links, password resets and order mail.
#
# ⭐ `chain` is the INTENDED PRODUCTION SETTING. Every provider with a usable free
#    tier here caps out in the low hundreds of messages a DAY — Brevo 300/day,
#    Resend 100/day and 3 000/month — and this platform's four notification stacks
#    spend them on ordinary traffic. Picking one just moves the ceiling; the chain
#    adds the allowances together and fails over the moment one says it has none
#    left, then returns to the primary when its window rolls over.
MAIL_PROVIDER=console

# Failover order for MAIL_PROVIDER=chain. Default: brevo,resend,smtp
#
# Each position is a decision, not an alphabetical accident:
#   brevo  first  — the largest daily free allowance (300/day), so it is the
#                   bucket worth spending before anything else is touched.
#   resend second — the reserve. It reports WHICH window it exhausted (daily vs
#                   monthly), so its latch releases at the right boundary with no
#                   configuration.
#   smtp   last   — no API quota, which argues for first until you notice the
#                   relay behind it is usually a personal mailbox whose limits are
#                   enforced by SILENTLY DROPPING mail. A provider whose ceiling
#                   cannot be observed is the wrong one to spend first.
#
# ⚠ A member with no credentials is SKIPPED with a warning, not a boot failure —
#   that is what lets one setting serve a laptop with no keys and a production
#   host with all three. A MISSPELT name is fatal.
# ⚠ `console` is refused as a member. It never fails, so it would absorb every
#   message the providers before it refused and report success.
# MAIL_PROVIDER_CHAIN=brevo,resend,smtp

# Per-request timeout for the HTTP providers (Brevo, Resend), in ms.
# MAIL_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=10000

# ── The quota latch ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# When a provider says its allowance is spent, the chain stops asking it until
# the allowance comes back. These two decide when that is.
#
# daily | monthly — which boundary a quota latch runs to WHEN THE PROVIDER DOES
# NOT SAY. Resend names the window it exhausted and overrides this per refusal;
# Brevo does not, so this is really "what period is my Brevo plan measured in".
#
# ⚠ Default `daily` because BREVO'S FREE TIER IS 300 PER DAY. Set `monthly` only
#   if you are on a Brevo plan with monthly credits — on a free account a monthly
#   latch means Brevo is used on one day and Resend carries the other thirty.
# MAIL_QUOTA_RESET_PERIOD=daily

# IANA zone the reset boundary is computed in.
# ⚠ Defaults to UTC, NOT the platform's Africa/Douala, and that is deliberate:
#   the boundary being modelled is the PROVIDER's accounting day, and both reckon
#   theirs in UTC. Pointing this at a local zone moves the guess away from the
#   real rollover and leaves the primary latched for an extra hour a day.
# MAIL_QUOTA_RESET_TIMEZONE=UTC

# How long a RATE LIMIT or an outage latches a provider, in ms. Deliberately
# separate from the quota boundary above: a 429-per-second or a 5xx clears in
# seconds, and latching one until midnight would throw away the primary's whole
# remaining allowance over a blip.
# MAIL_QUOTA_COOLDOWN_MS=300000

###############################################################################
#  10b. PHONE VERIFICATION  (WhatsApp OTP — `src/modules/phone-verification/`)
###############################################################################

# Vendors, agencies, agents and administrators sign up on a dashboard and may
# never message the bot, so they hold no WhatsApp CONNECTION — which is the proof
# /api/me/phone/confirm requires. Without this flow their phone_verified could
# never become true. Customers keep the stronger connection proof.

# How long a code stays redeemable.
# PHONE_VERIFY_TTL_SECONDS=600

# Wrong guesses before the code is destroyed.
# ⚠ THIS is the security of a 6-digit code, not its length: 5 attempts against
#   10^6 is 1-in-200,000 per code. Raising it to 50 makes it 1-in-20,000, which
#   looks like a usability tweak and is not.
# PHONE_VERIFY_MAX_ATTEMPTS=5

# Minimum gap between sends, per ACCOUNT (not per target number — a number-scoped
# gate bounds nothing when the attacker picks the number). Bounds both the paid
# template cost and the nuisance to whoever owns the number being tried.
# PHONE_VERIFY_RESEND_COOLDOWN_SECONDS=60

# The Meta template used OUTSIDE the 24-hour service window.
# ⚠ Must be category AUTHENTICATION, not UTILITY — submitting an OTP body as
#   UTILITY is a documented Meta rejection reason.
# ⛔ Measured 2026-09-14: this template is the ONE the submitter could not create.
#   The WABA now holds 188 templates; Meta refuses this one with code 10, because
#   AUTHENTICATION is gated behind business verification and the owning business is
#   `business_verification_status: "rejected"`. It is NOT the token — UTILITY creates
#   fine on the same credential. So the out-of-window path still does not work, for a
#   reason that lives in Business Manager rather than in this file or in the code.
#   Resolve the verification, then: npm run whatsapp:templates:submit -- --submit
#   Until then the UTILITY fallback below carries the code. This name stays set:
#   it is TRIED FIRST on every send, and the day it starts working it just works.
# PHONE_VERIFY_TEMPLATE_NAME=wi_mall_phone_verification

# The UTILITY template tried ONLY when the AUTHENTICATION one above fails to send.
#
# ⚠ This does not reverse the rule above — it answers a different problem. OTP content
#   still BELONGS in an AUTHENTICATION template, and that is still attempted first on
#   every single send. This exists because the category can be UNREACHABLE: without it
#   the out-of-window path is closed on this deployment no matter what the code does.
#
# ⛔ AND META REFUSED IT: INCORRECT_CATEGORY, both languages, 2026-09-14. This name
#   currently points at a REJECTED template, so the fallback send fails too and the
#   out-of-window path is closed either way. Left wired up because it costs one failing
#   API call and is correct the moment an approvable template exists.
#   Do NOT reword the copy to get past the classifier — resubmitting with
#   allow_category_change:true was rejected SYNCHRONOUSLY, so the route is closed by
#   Meta rather than by the wording, and the WABA holding the 188 working templates is
#   what would be at risk. Resolve the business verification instead; the AUTHENTICATION
#   path then resumes on its own and this stops being reached.
#
# ⚠ EMPTY CLOSES THE FALLBACK rather than disabling verification. The out-of-window
#   failure is then reported exactly as it was before this existed.
#
# ⚠ Invisible to clients on purpose — `delivery` stays `template` for either one, so no
#   frontend can come to depend on which template went out, and retiring the fallback
#   changes no response body. Which one was used is a server log line.
# PHONE_VERIFY_FALLBACK_TEMPLATE_NAME=wi_mall_phone_verification_utility

# ── Brand, injected into every email template ─────────────────────────────────
# The templates never hardcode the brand: `mail/domain/mail-brand.ts` merges these UNDER
# every caller's variables, so none of the eight send sites has to pass them and
# none can forget. Handlebars renders an unknown variable as the EMPTY STRING, so a
# forgotten brandName would silently produce "Welcome to ." rather than an error.
# BRAND_NAME=Wi-Mall

# The accent bar and every button. ⚠ The templates hardcode WHITE button text, so a
# light colour here fails contrast on the one element every transactional email has.
# The default (#4F46E5) is white-on-colour at ~6.4:1 — WCAG AA.
# BRAND_COLOR=#4F46E5

# Optional logo. Unset renders a typographic wordmark instead, which cannot fail to
# load and looks identical in every client.
# ⚠ Must be PUBLICLY reachable over https. A mail client fetches images from the
#   RECIPIENT's network, so a LAN or Tailscale URL is a broken image in every inbox —
#   the same trap as STORAGE_LOCAL_URL pointing at a 100.64/10 address.
# BRAND_LOGO_URL=https://wi-mall.com/logo.png

# The address printed in every email footer. Falls back to MAIL_FROM_DEFAULT.
# MAIL_SUPPORT_EMAIL=support@wi-mall.com

# ── Brevo (https://app.brevo.com → SMTP & API → API Keys) ────────────────────
# The v3 API key, used as the `api-key` header on POST /v3/smtp/email.
# Free tier: 300 emails/day. Exhaustion arrives as HTTP 402, or a body carrying
# `code: "not_enough_credits"` — the adapter accepts either as evidence.
#
# SOURCE: your Brevo account, SMTP & API → API Keys. Rotated by creating a new
#         key and deleting the old one; keys are not editable.
# ⚠ The sending domain must be authenticated in Brevo (SPF/DKIM) or Brevo
#   refuses with a DMARC/DNS error that names the domain.
# BREVO_API_KEY=
# BREVO_BASE_URL=https://api.brevo.com/v3

# ── Resend (https://resend.com/api-keys) ─────────────────────────────────────
# The API key, sent as `Authorization: Bearer re_…`.
# Free tier: 100/day AND 3 000/month. Resend names which one you hit
# (daily_quota_exceeded / monthly_quota_exceeded), which is why its latch needs
# no configuration.
#
# SOURCE: your Resend account, API Keys. A "sending"-scoped key is enough to
#         deliver but answers 401 on the GET /domains health probe.
# ⚠ Resend refuses to send from an UNVERIFIED domain (403). That is the single
#   commonest first-run failure here, and it is reported as a rejected MESSAGE
#   rather than a bad credential — rotating the key will not fix it.
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# RESEND_BASE_URL=https://api.resend.com

# ── SMTP ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SMTP credentials. For Gmail this is an App Password (Google Account →
# Security → 2-Step Verification → App passwords), NOT the account password.
#
# SOURCE: your mail provider. For Gmail, the App Password page above — it is
#         revoked and reissued there, never edited.
#         ⚠ SMTP_USER is deliberately absent from GET /system/config even
#         though it is not itself a secret; that was a human call the
#         credential-shaped regex could not make.
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_USER=
# SMTP_PASS=

# From-addresses per message class. They exist so transactional mail can be
# authenticated separately from marketing and land in different filters. Each
# falls back to a jovimall.com default that will fail SPF/DKIM on your domain.
# MAIL_FROM_DEFAULT=noreply@yourdomain.com
# MAIL_FROM_AUTH=auth@yourdomain.com
# MAIL_FROM_ORDER=orders@yourdomain.com
# MAIL_FROM_SYSTEM=system@yourdomain.com


###############################################################################
#  11. WHATSAPP  (Meta Cloud API — `src/modules/whatsapp/`)
###############################################################################

# From Meta for Developers → your app → WhatsApp → API Setup. The temporary
# token there expires in 24h; generate a permanent System User token for
# anything but a smoke test.
#
# BOTH must be set or the WhatsApp channel reports itself unconfigured and every
# notification handler silently skips it.
# SOURCE: Meta for Developers → your app → WhatsApp → API Setup. The token
#         there is temporary; a production one is a System User token from
#         Business Settings, and rotates in that console.
# WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN=
# WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=
#
# ⚠ WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID IS SHARED WITH n8n UNDER THE SAME NAME, and unlike
#   WHATSAPP_API_URL below the n8n side has NO FALLBACK: `send whatsapp` in
#   UP-wi-mall-core and `typing (first)` in UP-wi-mall-wa-adapter interpolate
#   `$env.WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID` raw. Setting it here alone leaves the customer
#   bot dialling the old id while this service dials the new one — one half of the
#   platform sends and the other does not, with nothing comparing them.
#
# ⚠ IT IS NOT STABLE ACROSS A NUMBER CHANGE, and the old id does not linger. When
#   +1 555-784-5447 was replaced by +237 652 705 926 on 2026-09-16 the old id
#   (614443908428263) was removed from the WABA outright and now answers Graph code
#   100 "object does not exist". A stale value is a dead send path, not a degraded one.
#   The WABA id is what survives a number change; the phone id is not.

# Graph API base. Bump the version deliberately — Meta deprecates on a schedule.
#
# ⚠ "Deliberately" means CHECK THE EXPIRY, not "leave it alone". v18.0 sat here until
#   2026-09-07 and had expired on 2026-01-26; Meta silently routes an expired version to the
#   oldest usable next one, so nothing broke and nothing said anything for eight months.
#   Each version lives roughly two years — https://developers.facebook.com/docs/graph-api/changelog
#
# SHARED with the n8n automation layer under THE SAME NAME. n8n's `send whatsapp` and
# `wa media meta` nodes read it as `$env.WHATSAPP_API_URL` (with this same literal as their
# fallback), so a bump is one edit here and one on the VPS.
# WHATSAPP_API_URL=https://graph.facebook.com/v26.0


###############################################################################
#  12. TELEGRAM  (`src/modules/telegram/`)
###############################################################################

# Shared secret the automation layer (n8n) must present as an `X-Webhook-Secret`
# header on POST /api/webhooks/whatsapp and POST /api/webhooks/telegram/webhook.
#
# Load-bearing since the `/connect` command: those endpoints MINT a connection
# code for whatever messaging identity the request names, so an open one lets
# anybody mint a code against a stranger's phone number, read it from the
# response, and attach that number to their own account.
#
# UNSET behaves differently by environment, deliberately:
#   production  → every bot webhook is REFUSED (401). /connect stops working.
#   development → webhooks stay open, with a warning at boot.
#
# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION. Generate with:
#     openssl rand -hex 32
#
# It is uncommented here because a template that leaves it commented is a
# template that deploys a production service whose bot webhooks 401 on every
# request — a loud failure, but one discovered by an operator rather than by
# this file. Setting it means setting THE SAME VALUE on the n8n side, as the
# `X-Webhook-Secret` header on both bot webhook nodes; the two halves are
# useless apart and there is no boot check that can see the far side.
#
# SOURCE: generated · openssl rand -hex 32
# SOURCE: SHARED with the n8n workflows as the `X-Webhook-Secret` header.
#         Not a repo-to-repo pair, so no test and no boot check can see the
#         far side — but it rotates in lockstep exactly like the four that are.
#         ../docs/RUNBOOK.md § Rotation.
BOT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=replace_me_openssl_rand_hex_32

# Signs the bot surface's SEALED IDENTITY TOKEN (`data.botToken` on
# /identity/resolve and /identity/sync). Falls back to JWT_SECRET when unset,
# so leaving it commented is a supported deployment rather than a broken one.
#
# WHAT IT IS FOR. An MCP-hosted tool cannot carry the `identity` envelope: n8n's
# MCP Server Trigger gives a connected tool node no query string, no headers,
# and executes AFTER the tool, so there is nothing to read it from. The model's
# arguments are the only channel — and a RAW envelope there would let any prompt
# injection name somebody else's chat id and read their cart. The sealed token
# is the one thing safe to put in front of a model: it can be echoed, never
# authored.
#
# ⚠ NOT A SHARED SECRET, and that is the point. jovi-mall both mints and
#   verifies; n8n only relays an opaque string. Nothing outside this service can
#   produce a token, so this is NOT one of the five cross-service values in
#   ../CLAUDE.md and it is absent from ../docs/RUNBOOK.md § Rotation on purpose.
#   Rotate it here alone: tokens minted before the restart stop verifying, which
#   costs one conversational turn and self-heals on the next /identity/sync.
#
# SOURCE: generated · openssl rand -hex 32 (a THIRD, different value)
# BOT_IDENTITY_TOKEN_SECRET=replace_me_a_third_openssl_rand_hex_32

# From @BotFather → /newbot. Unset disables the Telegram channel entirely.
# SOURCE: Telegram @BotFather → /mybots → API Token. Rotates with
#         /revoke there, which invalidates the old one immediately.
# TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
# The bot's @username without the @. Read by GET /api/me/connections to tell a
# user which bot to open and send `/connect` to; a wrong value points them at a
# bot that does not exist. Note the deep link only OPENS the chat — Telegram
# cannot pre-fill a message the way wa.me can, so the user types the command.
# TELEGRAM_BOT_NAME=

# ── Product cards, and the Telegram Mini App ─────────────────────────────────
#
# All four are OPTIONAL and each absence is a documented DEGRADATION, never a
# fault: the bot falls back to the next-simplest rendering and says nothing
# about it. Read `src/modules/bot-surface/domain/product-card.ts` before
# changing any of them — the failures they prevent are all silent ones.
#
# ⛔ THE FIRST TWO ARE A GO-LIVE STEP, and it is on the deploy checklist
#    rather than only here: ../docs/RUNBOOK.md § 0 and § "Configuration that
#    is inert until there is a real domain". Left unset on a real deployment
#    the bot still works, and its product cards have no pictures and no Mini
#    App — which reads as a design decision rather than a missing setting.

# The public origin of THIS API, as Telegram's and Meta's servers reach it.
#
# ⚠ AN ORIGIN, NOT A MEDIA PATH — https://api.yourdomain.com, never
#   https://api.yourdomain.com/api/files.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS. A product card's picture is fetched SERVER-SIDE by Telegram or
# by Meta, from the URL we hand them. The address this service knows itself by
# is routinely not one they can reach — a container hostname, a localhost, or a
# Tailscale 100.x address, which is what STORAGE_LOCAL_URL points at on the
# development box today. Card image URLs are rewritten onto this origin, and the
# no-product-image stand-in is served from it.
#
# UNSET falls back to API_PUBLIC_URL. When neither is publicly reachable the
# cards render WITHOUT PICTURES rather than failing — a Telegram sendPhoto with
# an unfetchable URL loses the caption and the buttons too, so the renderer
# checks first and degrades to text.
# BOT_MEDIA_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://api.yourdomain.com

# Where the Telegram Mini App is served from — normally the same origin as
# above, and it is a separate variable because a deployment may put the page
# behind a different edge.
#
# ⚠ MUST BE https://. Telegram refuses a `web_app` button on any other scheme,
#   and it refuses the WHOLE message — so a plain-http value here does not
#   produce a broken button, it produces a turn where the customer is told
#   nothing at all. The renderer checks the scheme and falls back to photo
#   cards, so this is safe to leave unset; it is simply the difference between
#   a scrollable page of products and a stack of images in the chat.
#
# The screens are served by this service at /api/bot/miniapp/s/<kind>/<handle>
# (the older single page at /api/bot/miniapp/p/<handle> still exists).
#
# NOTHING is registered in @BotFather for this. The buttons ride inside chat
# messages, which Telegram allows with no setup. BotFather's own "Mini App URL"
# field is one FIXED address and cannot carry the per-customer pass every screen
# needs, so leave it empty.
#
# ⚠ Telegram draws these buttons in PRIVATE chats with the bot only — not in a
#   group, and not on a message sent through a Telegram Business account. The
#   renderer checks the chat id: outside a private chat it sends an ordinary
#   link to the same screen, so the message still arrives. This platform never
#   sends through a Business account.
# BOT_MINIAPP_BASE_URL=https://api.yourdomain.com

# The approved WhatsApp CAROUSEL template, if this WABA has one.
#
# ⚠ A carousel on WhatsApp is not a message type — it is a MARKETING-category
#   template, pre-approved in Business Manager, and Meta will only send it with
#   the EXACT number of cards it was approved with. This platform's is five.
#   That is why a four-product answer takes the card path even here.
#
# The template must declare: one body variable (the intro sentence); five cards,
# each with an image header, three body variables (title, price, store) and two
# buttons — a quick reply, then a URL button whose href is
# `<STOREFRONT_URL>/shop/p/{{1}}` (the variable is the product id ALONE, because
# a template URL button takes a suffix and not a URL).
#
# UNSET is the ordinary case: every product list renders as interactive image
# cards, one message per product, which needs no approval and works inside the
# 24-hour service window.
# WHATSAPP_PRODUCT_CAROUSEL_TEMPLATE=
# The language code the template was approved under. Defaults to `en`.
# WHATSAPP_PRODUCT_CAROUSEL_LANG=en

# ── WhatsApp Flows — the encrypted data endpoint ─────────────────────────────
#
# A Flow is a FORM WhatsApp renders inside the chat. Unlike the Telegram Mini
# App it cannot be a web page: anything it shows that is not baked into the
# published form has to be fetched from us over a channel Meta encrypts end to
# end. These six variables are that channel.
#
# UNSET IS THE ORDINARY STATE AND IS NOT A FAULT. With no key the endpoint
# answers nothing and `channel-reply.ts` renders the `inapp` intent as a
# `cta_url` button instead — the same sentence, the same label, opening the
# storefront in a browser. That is the deployed behaviour today and it works.
#
# ⚠ DELIBERATELY DOES NOT FAIL THE BOOT WHEN ABSENT, unlike BOT_WEBHOOK_SECRET
#   and INTERNAL_ADMIN_SERVICE_TOKEN. Those guard a door; this describes a
#   capability the deployment may simply not have. Same posture as
#   GEO_TRACKER_ADMIN_TOKEN: absent means "no Flows here", and only a broken
#   value is a fault. A key that is SET AND UNPARSEABLE does refuse the boot,
#   via assertFlowKeyUsable() — because that is somebody who believes they
#   configured one.

# The private half of an RSA pair. Its PUBLIC half is uploaded to Meta; nothing
# here configures the public key, because it is DERIVED from this value — a
# separately-configured pair that does not match decrypts nothing while both
# halves look perfectly well-formed.
#
# ⚠ A PEM IS MULTI-LINE AND THIS FILE IS NOT. Escape the line breaks as \n.
#   Every path that carries this — a shell export, a compose `environment:`
#   entry, the Dokploy panel — flattens it, and a key that has lost its breaks
#   parses as nothing. Generate with:
#     openssl genrsa -out flow-private.pem 2048
# WHATSAPP_FLOW_PRIVATE_KEY=

# Only if the key above is passphrase-protected. An unencrypted key is fine.
# WHATSAPP_FLOW_PRIVATE_KEY_PASSPHRASE=

# The Meta App Secret, used to verify `X-Hub-Signature-256` on every request
# Meta posts to the endpoint.
#
# ⚠ THIS IS WHAT ACTUALLY AUTHENTICATES THE CALLER. The body being encrypted
#   proves only that the sender could encrypt to our PUBLIC key, and a public
#   key is not a secret. Unset means the signature is not checked at all —
#   acceptable for local work, wrong in production.
#
# ⚠ SET THIS WHENEVER THE FLOW KEY ABOVE IS SET, and not only for authenticity.
#   The endpoint lives under /api/webhooks/*, which is deliberately EXEMPT from
#   rate limiting (a 429 to a webhook sender is a lost event). An RSA private-key
#   decrypt is CPU-bound, so keys-configured-with-no-app-secret is the one
#   combination where an unauthenticated caller can spend this box's CPU at will
#   — on a 2 vCPU host sized for the whole platform. The signature check is a
#   cheap HMAC and the controller runs it BEFORE any RSA work, so setting it
#   closes that at no cost. Keys unset is safe either way: the endpoint answers
#   400 before it does any crypto at all.
#
# ⚠ SETTING THIS REQUIRES THE express.raw MOUNT IN app.ts for the Flows path.
#   The HMAC covers the exact bytes Meta sent, and a re-serialised parsed body
#   is not those bytes. Without the mount the endpoint REFUSES every request
#   rather than skipping the check — loudly, with a log line naming this cause.
# WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET=

# The published Flow id per screen, from Meta's Flow Builder. One variable per
# screen rather than a JSON map: test:env's census cannot see a name that is
# only ever a map key, and a malformed map would break every screen where a
# missing single id degrades exactly one.
#
# ⚠ A FLOW ID IS PER-ENVIRONMENT BY CONSTRUCTION. A Flow is published against
#   one WhatsApp Business Account, so development and production cannot share
#   one even in principle. Do not copy these between deployments.
#
# ⚠ A SCREEN WITH NO ID IS SIMPLY NOT OFFERED ON WHATSAPP — it falls back to the
#   browser link. Setting an id whose Flow is not published is worse than
#   leaving it unset: the customer opens the form and it fails inside Meta's UI,
#   where none of our copy reaches them.
# WHATSAPP_FLOW_ID_PRODUCT_LISTING=
# WHATSAPP_FLOW_ID_PRODUCT_DETAIL=
# WHATSAPP_FLOW_ID_CHECKOUT=


###############################################################################
#  13. PUSH NOTIFICATIONS  (FCM — `src/config/fcm.config.ts`)
#
#  A SEPARATE Firebase project from storage above, with its own credentials.
###############################################################################

# Master switch. When false, or when any credential is missing, push delivery
# degrades to a no-op — notifications still record in-app and still send over
# email/telegram/whatsapp.
# FCM_ENABLED=false

# Firebase console → Project settings → Service accounts → Generate new private
# key, on the MESSAGING project.
#
# SOURCE: Firebase console → Project settings → Service accounts (the MESSAGING
#         project, not the storage one). All three values come out of one
#         downloaded JSON key; rotate by issuing a new key there.
# FCM_PROJECT_ID=
# FCM_CLIENT_EMAIL=
# FCM_PRIVATE_KEY="-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\n...\n-----END PRIVATE KEY-----\n"


###############################################################################
#  14. PAYMENTS  (`src/modules/payments/`)
###############################################################################

# ── Stripe (cards) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# SOURCE: Stripe Dashboard → Developers → API keys (secret key) and
#         Developers → Webhooks → your endpoint → Signing secret.
#         Both roll in that dashboard. Stripe supports overlapping keys during
#         a roll, so this one needs no downtime window.
#
# From the Stripe Dashboard → Developers → API keys.
# The WiMall Stripe account is Canadian and settles in USD: charges are created
# in STRIPE_CHARGE_CURRENCY and the XAF catalogue price is converted at charge
# time using the fixed rate below. Mobile money keeps charging native XAF.
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_...
#
# The PUBLISHABLE key, which src/ DOES read as of GAP-008 (2026-08-26). This
# note used to say the opposite — "deliberately absent: nothing in src/ reads
# it" — and that stopped being true when the hosted card page landed.
#
# It is served to an anonymous browser by GET /api/payments/session/:token, so
# the hosted page can mount the Stripe Payment Element. That is what the key is
# for; Stripe puts it in its own client-side snippets, and on its own it can do
# nothing without a client secret.
#
# ⚠ Putting a SECRET key here is the one mistake with an unbounded blast radius,
# and the two values differ by a few characters. `stripePublishableKey()`
# refuses any value starting `sk_` or `rk_` and logs an error rather than
# publishing it — card pages then report cards as unconfigured until it is
# corrected. Do not "fix" that by relaxing the check.
#
# Optional: unset means this deployment takes no cards on a hosted page. Mobile
# money is unaffected — it completes on the customer's handset and needs no page.
# STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=pk_test_...

# From `stripe listen` (local) or Dashboard → Developers → Webhooks → your
# endpoint → Signing secret.
# REQUIRED whenever STRIPE_SECRET_KEY is set: without it no signature can be
# verified, so no payment is ever confirmed — customers are charged and their
# orders stay unpaid. The boot validator refuses this combination.
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...

# Presentment currency for all Stripe charges.
# STRIPE_CHARGE_CURRENCY=usd
# Fixed conversion: how many XAF to 1 USD. A stale rate silently over- or
# under-charges every card customer.
# STRIPE_XAF_PER_USD=600

# ── The hosted card page (GAP-008) ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# How long a minted payment link lives, in minutes.
#
# Default 30, matched to the checkout stock hold: a reservation lapses at thirty
# minutes, so a link outliving it would open a payment page for goods no longer
# held. The remedy for an expired link is a new one, which costs one call.
#
# The link itself is {STOREFRONT_URL}/pay/{token} — so STOREFRONT_URL is what
# decides whether a link can be built at all. Unset there means the mint answers
# `url: null` and the automation layer must offer mobile money instead.
# PAYMENT_LINK_TTL_MINUTES=30

# ── NotchPay (mobile money) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# SOURCE: NotchPay dashboard → Settings → API keys. Three DISTINCT keys with
#         three distinct jobs (see below) — rotating them means replacing all
#         three together, since the dashboard reissues the set.
#         ⚠ ACCOUNT-LEVEL, not configurable here: this merchant account cannot
#         use refunds (POST /refunds answers 403), which is why
#         NOTCHPAY_REFUNDS_ENABLED defaults to false.
#
# All three from the NotchPay dashboard → Settings → API keys. They are THREE
# DISTINCT KEYS WITH THREE DISTINCT JOBS and are not interchangeable — the wrong
# one in the wrong variable fails in a way that reads as a network fault.
#
# The public key (pk_…): sent as the `Authorization` header on every call.
# Renamed from NOTCHPAY_API_KEY, which could no longer say which key it meant.
# NOTCHPAY_PUBLIC_KEY=pk_test....
#
# The private key (sk_…): sent as `X-Grant`, and ONLY on sensitive endpoints —
# for us that is refunds. Collection works without it; a refund refuses.
# NOTCHPAY_PRIVATE_KEY=sk_test....
#
# The Hash Key (hsk_…): verifies inbound callbacks — HMAC-SHA256 over the raw
# body, presented in `x-notch-signature`. NOT the private key.
# REQUIRED whenever NOTCHPAY_PUBLIC_KEY is set, and the boot validator refuses
# the combination: without it every callback is refused, so customers are
# charged and their orders stay unpaid. Use the sandbox hash for test webhooks
# and the production hash for live ones — they are different values.
# NOTCHPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=hsk_test....
#
# NOTCHPAY_BASE_URL=https://api.notchpay.co
# NOTCHPAY_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=15000
#
# Whether refunds are enabled ON THE MERCHANT ACCOUNT — a different question
# from whether we implement them (we do).
#
# ⚠ Default FALSE, because that is what the account actually does. Verified
# against the live sandbox 2026-08-18: `GET /refunds` answers 200 with these
# credentials while `POST /refunds` answers a bare `{"code":"403"}` for every
# body shape. Leaving it true would offer an administrator a refund button that
# fails the instant it is pressed, which is exactly what the up-front
# `gatewayRefundSupported` verdict exists to prevent. A NotchPay refund
# meanwhile degrades to the manual-payout ticket, same as My-CoolPay.
#
# Ask NotchPay to enable refunds, then flip this. No code changes with it.
# NOTCHPAY_REFUNDS_ENABLED=false
#
# Whether PAYOUTS (money out, `POST /transfers`) are enabled for this deployment.
#
# ⚠ Default FALSE, and turning it on is an OPS step as much as a config change. Three
# things must all be true before a transfer can succeed, and only the first is in this file:
#
#   1. NOTCHPAY_PAYOUTS_ENABLED=true
#   2. NOTCHPAY_PRIVATE_KEY is set — the X-Grant credential. The boot validator now
#      REFUSES to start without it when this is true, because a missing X-Grant answers
#      403, which is the same status as (3) and sends you looking in the wrong place.
#   3. This server's PUBLIC EGRESS IP is registered with NotchPay:
#        business.notchpay.co → Settings → Developer → IPs
#      Transfers are IP-allowlisted; collection is not. So a deployment that has been
#      taking money happily for months will refuse every payout until this is done, and
#      will do it with a 403 that reads like revoked credentials. Re-register whenever the
#      host moves. My-CoolPay has the identical trap; see its block below.
#
# With it FALSE, payouts still work — an administrator sends the money by hand and records
# an external reference through mark-paid. That path is kept deliberately and is the only
# way to settle a BANK or CARD destination, which no gateway here can reach.
# NOTCHPAY_PAYOUTS_ENABLED=false
#
# ⚠ The webhook URL below serves TRANSFERS as well as payments — do not register a second
# one. NotchPay signs both with the same Hash Key and the handler routes on the event type
# (`transfer.*` vs `payment.*`). Subscribe the transfer events in the dashboard:
#   transfer.created · transfer.complete · transfer.failed
#
# Webhook endpoint to register in the dashboard:
#   https://<your-public-host>/api/webhooks/notchpay
# It must be HTTPS with a valid certificate; localhost is rejected. In
# development use a tunnel (cloudflared/ngrok) and re-point the dashboard each
# time the tunnel URL changes.

# ── My-CoolPay (mobile money) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# SOURCE: My-CoolPay merchant dashboard → your app → keys.
#         ⚠ ACCOUNT-LEVEL: payouts are IP-ALLOWLISTED, so rotating a key is
#         not enough if the host also changes — the new egress IP has to be
#         registered with them first or every payout fails.
#         ⚠ This is LIVE MONEY in the current configuration.
#
# The public key goes in the URL PATH, not a header:
# `https://my-coolpay.com/api/{public_key}/payin`. It is also echoed back on
# every callback as `application` and is checked against this value.
# Renamed from MYCOOLPAY_API_KEY.
# MYCOOLPAY_PUBLIC_KEY=
#
# The private key does DOUBLE DUTY: it signs the callback (MD5 over
# transaction_ref + type + amount + currency + operator + this key) and it
# authorises payout/balance via `X-PRIVATE-KEY`.
# REQUIRED whenever MYCOOLPAY_PUBLIC_KEY is set — the boot validator refuses the
# combination, because without it every callback is refused and no mobile-money
# payment through this gateway ever settles.
#
# ⚠ There is NO `MYCOOLPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET`. That name was documented here for a
# while and read by nothing: the provider has no such credential. It is listed
# in the validator's RENAMED_VARS so an operator who set it is told.
# MYCOOLPAY_PRIVATE_KEY=
#
# MYCOOLPAY_BASE_URL=https://my-coolpay.com/api
# MYCOOLPAY_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=15000
#
# My-CoolPay publishes a single callback source address (15.236.140.89) and
# their own SDK pins it. OFF by default here: behind a proxy or a tunnel
# `req.ip` is the hop rather than the origin, so switching it on without the
# matching `trust proxy` configuration refuses every genuine callback.
# MYCOOLPAY_VERIFY_CALLBACK_IP=false
#
# Callback URL to register in the My-CoolPay dashboard:
#   https://<your-public-host>/api/webhooks/mycoolpay
# HTTPS with a valid SSL certificate is enforced by the provider.

# ── Reconciliation and the OTP step ──────────────────────────────────────────
# A mobile-money confirmation arrives minutes after the request that started it,
# and the customer has usually closed the page — so a dropped callback needs a
# sweep, not a client poll. This worker re-verifies locally-pending mobile-money
# transactions (and pending plan purchases / credit top-ups) against the
# gateway's own record.
#
# MIN_AGE is a settling window: re-verifying a 30-second-old transaction only
# spends gateway quota. MAX_AGE bounds the sweep — anything older belongs to
# `npm run audit:stuck-payments`, not to a job that runs every ten minutes.
# PAYMENT_RECONCILE_CRON=*/10 * * * *
# PAYMENT_RECONCILE_MIN_AGE_MINUTES=10
# PAYMENT_RECONCILE_MAX_AGE_HOURS=72
# PAYMENT_RECONCILE_BATCH_SIZE=50
#
# Wrong one-time codes tolerated before the payment is failed. My-CoolPay's
# Orange Money flow answers REQUIRE_OTP and the endpoint that accepts the code
# is unauthenticated (it sits beside /initiate and /verify, which are open by
# design for shareable payment links), so this counter is what stands between a
# caller and a six-digit code.
# PAYMENT_OTP_MAX_ATTEMPTS=5

# Admin `users._id` recorded as the author of system-raised dispute/chargeback
# tickets and system-driven earnings actions. Unset, dispute handling still runs
# but no review ticket is opened — the chargeback is processed and nobody is
# told. Get it from the users collection.
# SUPPORT_ADMIN_USER_ID=


###############################################################################
#  15. EARNINGS, ESCROW & PAYOUTS  (`src/modules/earnings/`)
#
#  Money values are INTEGERS in minor currency units, matching
#  `order.total_amount`.
###############################################################################

# ISO-4217 code stamped on newly created accounts and allocations.
# EARNINGS_CURRENCY=XAF

# Days funds are held after an order completes before moving from pending to
# available. Every actor on an order matures on the same date.
# EARNINGS_HOLD_DAYS=7

# Days a delivered order may sit unconfirmed by the customer before the system
# confirms it (which then starts the hold clock above).
# EARNINGS_AUTO_CONFIRM_DAYS=7

# The per-SHIPMENT analogue, and the system is unpayable without it:
# `agent_delivered → delivered` is otherwise only triggered by the customer, so
# one silent customer freezes the whole order's escrow for every actor forever.
# EARNINGS_SHIPMENT_AUTO_CONFIRM_DAYS=7

# Defensive fallback delivery fee used ONLY when a shipment's agency has no
# pricing policy configured. Defaults to 0 — charge nothing rather than a
# fabricated number.
# EARNINGS_DELIVERY_FLAT_FEE=0
c
# Daily release / auto-confirm sweep (node-cron) and its per-stage batch cap.
# EARNINGS_CRON=0 1 * * *
# EARNINGS_BATCH_SIZE=200

# Floor on a payout request, manual or automatic. Below it both the endpoint and
# the sweep refuse with EARNINGS_PAYOUT_BELOW_MINIMUM.
# EARNINGS_MIN_PAYOUT_AMOUNT=10000

# Available balance at which the platform opens a payout request on the owner's
# behalf, so balances never grow unbounded into money the platform owes.
# EARNINGS_AUTO_PAYOUT_THRESHOLD=2000000

# Payout ALLOWANCE for an owner whose KYC is not verified, measured over a rolling
# window (below). Anything above the remaining allowance stays in available_balance.
#   0 = NO CAP, and it is the default: this feature is INERT until you set a
#   number. Deliberate for a money path — a live default would start capping
#   payouts on the deploy that shipped it, with nobody having chosen the figure.
# ⚠ Keep it above EARNINGS_MIN_PAYOUT_AMOUNT. Set below the floor it blocks
#   unverified payouts entirely (the capped amount then fails the minimum); the
#   refusal names the cap as the cause, but the configuration is still wrong.
# ⚠ The auto-threshold sweep above is EXEMPT — capping it would leave the platform
#   owing more to the least-vetted accounts, which is the opposite of its purpose.
# EARNINGS_UNVERIFIED_PAYOUT_CAP=0

# The rolling window the allowance above is measured over, in days.
# ⚠ A PER-REQUEST cap bounds nothing: only one payout may be PENDING at a time, but
#   the moment an admin marks it paid the owner opens another. At a 20,000 cap an
#   unverified owner with 200,000 available just requests ten times.
# ⚠ ROLLING, not calendar: a calendar reset lets the 31st plus the 1st move twice the
#   cap in 48 hours, which is the burst the cap exists to stop.
# Only `paid` requests count, windowed on resolved_at — a REJECTED request returned
# the money to available_balance and must not be charged to the owner.
# EARNINGS_UNVERIFIED_PAYOUT_WINDOW_DAYS=30


###############################################################################
#  16. CASH ON DELIVERY  (`src/modules/cod/`)
###############################################################################

# Delivery-code (OTP) verification: wrong attempts before the code locks and
# must be regenerated, and the minimum gap between re-sends.
# COD_OTP_MAX_ATTEMPTS=5
# COD_OTP_RESEND_MIN_SECONDS=60

# Default ceiling on the cash one agent may be exposed to — held plus expected
# from assigned uncollected COD shipments. Per-contract overrides narrow it.
# COD_AGENT_MAX_EXPOSURE_DEFAULT=300000

# Trust bands on the 0–100 agent score. At/above FULL the agent gets their whole
# exposure limit; between REDUCED and FULL they get half; below REDUCED, COD is
# blocked entirely. REDUCED must not exceed FULL or the middle band is empty.
# COD_TRUST_FULL_THRESHOLD=80
# COD_TRUST_REDUCED_THRESHOLD=50

# Score deducted for a late deposit and for a cash shortfall.
# COD_TRUST_PENALTY_LATE_DEPOSIT=5
# COD_TRUST_PENALTY_SHORTFALL=20

# Days an agent may hold collected cash before the sweep opens a `late_deposit`
# discrepancy and applies the penalty above.
# COD_DEPOSIT_DEADLINE_DAYS=2

# Days an agency has to confirm or reject a deposit its agent declared.
# MUST NOT EXCEED COD_DEPOSIT_DEADLINE_DAYS — a declaration suppresses the
# agent's own late-deposit penalty, so a longer window here is a free way for an
# agent to park an unfalsifiable claim and stop their clock. The boot validator
# enforces this.
# COD_DEPOSIT_CONFIRM_DEADLINE_DAYS=2

# Daily deposit-deadline sweep. 01:00, 02:00, 03:00 and 05:00 are taken by
# other workers; none of them has an overlap guard, so keep them apart.
# COD_DEPOSIT_SWEEP_CRON=0 4 * * *

# Rolling reserve on agency COD earnings: the percentage of each released COD
# allocation diverted, and the days it matures before releasing.
# COD_RESERVE_PERCENT=10
# COD_RESERVE_DAYS=30

# COD_BATCH_SIZE=200


###############################################################################
#  17. DELIVERY AGENTS  (`src/modules/agents/config/agent.config.ts`)
###############################################################################

# Bounds on an agent's OWN global COD threshold — the total cash they may hold
# across every agency combined. The agent sets their value within these.
# AGENT_COD_THRESHOLD_MAX=5000000
# AGENT_COD_THRESHOLD_MIN=0

# Bounds on a SINGLE contract's COD threshold, which is a sub-allocation of the
# agent's global one and is therefore bounded twice.
# AGENCY_AGENT_COD_THRESHOLD_MAX=1000000
# AGENCY_AGENT_COD_THRESHOLD_MIN=0

# Bounds on `capacity.max_active_shipments`. The live value is PLAN-DRIVEN (the
# agent's pricing plan writes it); MAX is the ceiling any plan or admin may set,
# DEFAULT matches the free tier so a plan-less agent behaves like a free
# subscriber. Capacity is agent-level and shared across every agency.
# AGENT_MAX_ACTIVE_SHIPMENTS_MAX=100
# AGENT_MAX_ACTIVE_SHIPMENTS_MIN=1
# AGENT_MAX_ACTIVE_SHIPMENTS_DEFAULT=20

# How many allocating contracts one agent may hold at once.
# AGENT_MAX_AGENCY_RELATIONSHIPS=5

# Trust composite weights. Business policy, not derived. THEY MUST SUM TO 100 —
# `agent.config.ts` throws at import if they do not, because a silently
# rescaled score is worse than a loud failure. Note this check runs before the
# environment validator, so it is the first thing you will see.
# AGENT_TRUST_WEIGHT_COD=30
# AGENT_TRUST_WEIGHT_ACTIVITY=20
# AGENT_TRUST_WEIGHT_CUSTOMER=30
# AGENT_TRUST_WEIGHT_AGENCY=10
# AGENT_TRUST_WEIGHT_VENDOR=10

# Score for an agent with no signals at all. A new agent is trusted by default;
# seeding at 0 would lock every new agent out of COD.
# AGENT_TRUST_SCORE_SEED=100

# Observations before a rating factor is trusted alone. Below this it is blended
# toward the seed, so one angry customer cannot define a reputation.
# AGENT_TRUST_MIN_OBSERVATIONS=5

# Cash volume (minor units) at which the COD track record earns full credit for
# scale — returning 5M cleanly is stronger evidence than returning 5k cleanly.
# AGENT_TRUST_COD_VOLUME_FULL_CREDIT=5000000

# Nightly trust recompute. Recompute is batch-only by product decision.
# AGENT_TRUST_RECOMPUTE_CRON=0 3 * * *
# AGENT_TRUST_RECOMPUTE_ENABLED=true

# ── PLAN QUOTA (enforcing plan limits after a downgrade) ─────────────────────

# Plan limits used to bind only at creation time, so a vendor who downgraded kept every
# product live and every byte served. The plan-quota module recomputes what fits, OLDEST
# FIRST, and suspends products / blocks files past the allowance — never deleting either.
#
# Enforcement itself is NOT configurable and has no kill switch: it runs on every plan
# change, through the `plan.activated` consumer. The two settings below govern only the
# nightly DRIFT SWEEP, which is the durability half — it re-runs enforcement for owners
# whose stamped plan no longer matches their live one (a lost event, an owner never
# enforced, or an administrator editing a plan in place, which changes no plan id and
# emits no event at all) and for owners currently holding something back (so "archive
# something older and the next item returns" is true even though archiving changes no
# plan).
#
# Runs after plan-expiry (0 3) and the agency shipment-cap monitor (30 3), so it sees the
# result of the same night's expiries rather than the previous night's.
# PLAN_QUOTA_RECONCILE_CRON=45 3 * * *

# Disables the drift sweep ONLY. Enforcement on plan change still happens. Set false while
# investigating an incident; leaving it false indefinitely means a lost event is never
# repaired.
# PLAN_QUOTA_RECONCILE_ENABLED=true

# ── AGENCY INVENTORY (Phase 6 · Step 14) ─────────────────────────────────────

# Rebuilds every agency's stored-SKU roster from the catalogue, and repairs any depot
# counter that has drifted from its own movement ledger. This work used to run debounced
# on the inventory READ path; counted stock made that the wrong place for it — the pass now
# walks the ledger, and an agency nobody is looking at is the one whose drift wants finding.
# Fifteen minutes because the roster is what an agency records intake against.
# AGENCY_INVENTORY_RECONCILE_CRON=*/15 * * * *
# AGENCY_INVENTORY_RECONCILE_ENABLED=true

# Issues last month's storage statements, on the 1st. A RECORD of rent owed per
# (agency, vendor) — nothing here moves money, and nothing should be added that does
# without reopening that decision. Idempotent on (agency, vendor, period), so a missed run
# is safe to trigger by hand from /api/internal/admin/dev-tools/workers.
# AGENCY_STORAGE_INVOICE_CRON=0 2 1 * *
# AGENCY_STORAGE_INVOICE_ENABLED=true

# Nightly reconciliation of agent capacity counters against real shipment counts.
# AGENT_CAPACITY_RECONCILE_CRON=0 4 * * *

# Nightly convergence of every agent's COD pool onto plan × KYC verdict × admin pin.
# The fast path is event-driven; this catches lost events and converges agents written
# before the rule existed. Triggerable from /api/internal/admin/dev-tools/workers —
# worth running once right after the deploy that introduces it.
# AGENT_COD_POOL_RECONCILE_CRON=30 4 * * *

# Whether an agent must have device location confirmed ON to receive shipments.
# geo-tracker is the only component that can observe this. Defaults OFF: turning
# it on before geo-tracker reports device state makes EVERY agent permanently
# ineligible and dispatch stops platform-wide.
# AGENT_REQUIRE_DEVICE_LOCATION=false

# How an UNKNOWN device-location signal (never reported / provider offline) is
# treated when the flag above is on. `allow` fails open so a geo-tracker outage
# does not halt dispatch; `deny` fails closed. An explicit `false` from the
# device always blocks, under either policy.
# AGENT_UNKNOWN_DEVICE_LOCATION_POLICY=allow

# Age past which a mirrored tracking state is shown as stale rather than live.
# Business reference only — geo-tracker owns the real liveness signal, and no
# assignment rule reads the mirror.
# AGENT_TRACKING_STATE_STALE_AFTER_SECONDS=120

# Tracking Allow for a newly created agent. Note that `false` makes every new
# agent undispatchable until an administrator flips the flag per agent —
# `assertEligible` requires tracking-allowed before dispatch.
# AGENT_TRACKING_ALLOWED_BY_DEFAULT=true


###############################################################################
#  18. SHIPMENT ASSIGNMENT & AUTO-ASSIGNMENT
#      (`src/modules/shipment-assignment/config/assignment.config.ts`)
###############################################################################

# How long an agent has to accept an offer. PLATFORM-WIDE by product decision —
# there is deliberately no per-agency override. Seconds.
# SHIPMENT_OFFER_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=120

# The expiry sweep's cadence and per-tick cap. Wants to be well under the
# timeout above, or an ignored offer survives an extra cycle before the next
# candidate is tried.
# SHIPMENT_OFFER_EXPIRY_SWEEP_INTERVAL_MS=30000
# SHIPMENT_OFFER_EXPIRY_SWEEP_BATCH=100

# How many ranked candidates are snapshotted onto an auto-assignment session.
# The broadcast walks this list without recomputing, so it also caps how many
# agents one auto-assignment will ever try.
# SHIPMENT_ASSIGNMENT_MAX_CANDIDATES=20

# Full passes over that ranking before the agency is told automatic assignment
# failed. Round 2 re-nudges the agents who ignored, never the ones who rejected.
# SHIPMENT_ASSIGNMENT_MAX_ROUNDS=2

# Minimum trust score to receive ANY auto-assigned shipment. 0 leaves the floor
# inert. COD carries its own stricter cash gate on top.
# SHIPMENT_ASSIGNMENT_MIN_TRUST_SCORE=0

# When true, ONLY a live position fresher than POSITION_FRESHNESS_SECONDS counts
# as "location available", and agents without one are dropped from the pool —
# which includes EVERY agent who has never had a tracked delivery.
# When false, a stale position or the declared home base ranks the agent, and an
# agent with no position at all is ranked last instead of dropped.
# SHIPMENT_ASSIGNMENT_REQUIRE_LIVE_POSITION=false
# SHIPMENT_ASSIGNMENT_POSITION_FRESHNESS_SECONDS=300

# Ranking weights. NOT required to sum to 100 — each factor is normalised to
# 0..1 first and the weighted sum is renormalised, so these are pure relative
# importance. COD is not a weight: it is a hard filter.
# SHIPMENT_ASSIGNMENT_WEIGHT_DISTANCE=50
# SHIPMENT_ASSIGNMENT_WEIGHT_CAPACITY=20
# SHIPMENT_ASSIGNMENT_WEIGHT_TRUST=30

# Distance normalisation, in km: at or under FULL scores 1, at or over ZERO
# scores 0, linear between. UNKNOWN is the neutral score used when either set of
# coordinates is missing, so a missing location degrades rather than always
# losing.
# SHIPMENT_ASSIGNMENT_DISTANCE_FULL_KM=1
# SHIPMENT_ASSIGNMENT_DISTANCE_ZERO_KM=25
# SHIPMENT_ASSIGNMENT_UNKNOWN_DISTANCE_SCORE=0.5

# geo-tracker routing paths used for proximity ranking. Both are OFF the
# critical path: any failure, or an unset GEO_TRACKER_BASE_URL, falls back to
# local haversine, so auto-assignment never blocks on geo-tracker.
# SHIPMENT_ASSIGNMENT_GEO_MATRIX_PATH=/routing/matrix
# SHIPMENT_ASSIGNMENT_GEO_ROUTE_PATH=/routing/route
# SHIPMENT_ASSIGNMENT_GEO_TIMEOUT_MS=3000

# Claims on the short-lived service JWT minted for those routing calls.
# geo-tracker verifies it with the SHARED JWT_SECRET and reads { userId, role };
# the routing endpoints admit any authenticated caller.
# SHIPMENT_ASSIGNMENT_GEO_TOKEN_SUBJECT=jovi-mall-assignment
# SHIPMENT_ASSIGNMENT_GEO_TOKEN_ROLE=admin
# SHIPMENT_ASSIGNMENT_GEO_TOKEN_TTL_SECONDS=60

# on_accept | on_offer — when an offer reveals the customer's full name, phone
# and street address.
# `on_accept` (the default) is deliberate: auto-assignment BROADCASTS one offer
# to several agents at once and keeps earlier offers standing, so `on_offer`
# hands a customer's PII to every agent who was offered the job, including all
# the ones who declined.
# SHIPMENT_ASSIGNMENT_OFFER_PII_REVEAL=on_accept


###############################################################################
#  19. LIVE TRACKING — geo-tracker integration ("Project B")
#      (`src/modules/tracking-integration/`)
#
#  jovi-mall stays the source of truth and PUSHES shipment-lifecycle events, so
#  an agency or customer loses live tracking the moment a shipment finishes.
###############################################################################

# geo-tracker base URL. EMPTY DISABLES THE INTEGRATION ENTIRELY — the outbox is
# still written but the dispatcher no-ops, so a deployment without geo-tracker
# runs unaffected. That is the intended local default, not a bug.
GEO_TRACKER_BASE_URL=

# HMAC-SHA256 signing key for every outbound event.
# MUST EQUAL geo-tracker's WEBHOOK_HMAC_SECRET (same value, different variable
# name on each side). Required whenever the base URL above is set: without it
# every dispatched event is rejected and the outbox fills with failed rows.
# Generate with `openssl rand -hex 32`.
#
# SOURCE: generated · openssl rand -hex 32
# SOURCE: SHARED with geo-tracker as WEBHOOK_HMAC_SECRET  ⚠ LOCKSTEP, AND THE
#         TWO NAMES DIFFER. This is the trap the rotation procedure was written
#         for: two names for one value is exactly the shape that survives a
#         rotation of one side. Unlike JWT_SECRET, a mismatch here is at least
#         visible — the outbox fills with failed rows rather than failing
#         silently. ../docs/RUNBOOK.md § Rotation.
GEO_TRACKER_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Dispatcher tuning. INTERVAL is the outbox drain cadence, BATCH the events per
# drain, MAX_ATTEMPTS the tries before a row is parked as `failed` (it is then
# only recoverable through the admin replay endpoint).
# GEO_TRACKER_DISPATCH_INTERVAL_MS=2000
# GEO_TRACKER_DISPATCH_BATCH_SIZE=50
# GEO_TRACKER_MAX_ATTEMPTS=10
# GEO_TRACKER_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=5000

# Ceiling on the agency live-tracking board's snapshot. A live map load, not a
# paginated list — this is a bound against a pathological account. When it
# bites, the response reports meta.truncated.
# TRACKING_BOARD_MAX_SHIPMENTS=200

# Tracking Allow reconciliation — the BACKSTOP for the one cross-service event
# with no other recovery path (plan step 3.A.3).
#
# An administrator's tracking REVOCATION reaches geo-tracker as an outbox row
# written in the same transaction as the flag, so it cannot be lost in a crash.
# What it can still hit is delivery failure: MAX_ATTEMPTS above parks the row as
# `failed`, and nothing replays it without an operator. Every OTHER event type
# survives that — geo-tracker's agentHasActiveShipment aggregate eventually
# closes a session the per-shipment verdict missed. This one has no aggregate,
# no sweep and no TTL, and its failure mode is the worst in the contract: the
# administrator was shown success and the agent goes on broadcasting a live
# position indefinitely. This sweep re-pushes revocations until they land.
#
# INTERVAL is therefore a backstop cadence, not a delivery latency (the real
# push is immediate). Do not raise it above the dispatcher's backoff-to-parked
# window, or it stops being a backstop and becomes a second timer. BATCH is a
# ceiling against a pathological population — a migration that revoked
# everybody — not a page size: passes run oldest-decision-first, so a larger
# revoked set is covered across successive passes rather than flooding one.
#
# Inert when GEO_TRACKER_BASE_URL is unset, like the dispatcher it feeds.
# Grants are deliberately NOT re-pushed: a lost grant leaves an agent
# un-locatable, which fails in the safe direction and self-heals.
# TRACKING_ALLOW_RECONCILE_INTERVAL_MS=900000
# TRACKING_ALLOW_RECONCILE_BATCH=200

# NOTE: geo-tracker must also run with JWT_SECRET identical to this service's —
# it verifies the same HS256 access tokens. See ../CLAUDE.md for the full
# cross-service contract.


###############################################################################
#  20. GEOCODING  (`src/core/geocoding/`)
#
#  Turns free-form address text into a stored GeoAddress. Swapping providers is
#  a config change only — no business logic branches on the provider.
###############################################################################

# nominatim | google | mapbox | here | geoapify
# ⚠ ONLY `nominatim` has an adapter in this build. The factory throws
# GEO_PROVIDER_NOT_CONFIGURED for the others, and the boot validator now
# refuses them outright rather than letting the failure land on a customer's
# first address search.
# GEO_PROVIDER=nominatim

# GEO_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=5000
# Candidates returned per search.
# GEO_DEFAULT_LIMIT=5
# Comma-separated ISO-3166-1 alpha-2 codes biasing results. Blank = worldwide.
# GEO_DEFAULT_COUNTRY_CODES=cm

# ── Nominatim (default, keyless) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# GEO_NOMINATIM_BASE_URL=https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org
# The OSM usage policy REQUIRES an identifying User-Agent and blocks callers
# that do not send one. Set it to something that identifies this deployment.
# GEO_NOMINATIM_USER_AGENT=jovi-mall/1.0 (+https://yourdomain.com; geocoding)
# Contact address, also part of the usage policy for heavier use.
# GEO_NOMINATIM_EMAIL=ops@yourdomain.com

# ── The paid providers, and the failover chain (ADR-A04 D-2) ─────────────────
#
# Every provider with a usable free tier caps out in the low thousands of calls a
# day, and address search at checkout is exactly the traffic that spends them. So
# the production setting is not a provider — it is `chain`, which ADDS the
# allowances together and fails over on a 429 or an outage.
#
#   GEO_PROVIDER=chain
#   GEO_PROVIDER_CHAIN=locationiq,geoapify
#
# ORDER IS CHOSEN ON RELEVANCE, NOT ON RATE LIMITS — and the rate-limit argument
# says the opposite, which is why this note is long. See ADR-A04 D-3's amendment.
#
# The chain only asks the next provider when the first returns NOTHING. So a
# first provider that answers CONFIDENTLY AND WRONGLY is never corrected, while
# one that 429s always is: the failover already absorbs the rate limit, and
# nothing absorbs bad relevance.
#
# Measured on six real Cameroonian addresses (2026-08-23), top result vs. truth:
# LocationIQ within 10 km 6/6, Geoapify 5/6 — and the miss was 267 km, a
# same-named town, with the exact street ranked THIRD at confidence 0. That is a
# RANKING defect, not a coverage gap, so no second provider and no adapter change
# fixes it.
#
# Accepted cost: LocationIQ's 2 rps is now the front line, so busy typing 429s
# more often and each 429 wastes one call before Geoapify answers. Affordable
# because the result cache below sits in front of the WHOLE chain. Revisit if
# GEO_PROVIDER_RATE_LIMITED becomes common in the logs.
#
# `nominatim` is ALWAYS appended as the last resort and never needs naming. It is
# keyless, so the chain can never come out empty and a deployment with no keys at
# all still resolves an address.
#
# A provider whose key is absent is SKIPPED with a warning, not a boot failure —
# that is what lets one `GEO_PROVIDER=chain` setting serve a laptop with no keys,
# staging with one, and production with both. A MISSPELT name in the chain is
# fatal, deliberately: silently skipping it is how a deployment runs on its
# fallback believing it runs on its primary.
#
# ⚠ The chain never appears in stored data. `GeoAddress.provider` records the
# service that actually resolved the address, so a row reads `geoapify` or
# `locationiq`, never `chain` — that is what keeps `provider_place_id` resolvable.
# GEO_PROVIDER_CHAIN=locationiq,geoapify

# Geoapify — https://myprojects.geoapify.com (free signup, NO credit card).
# Free tier: 3 000 credits/day, up to 5 requests/second, SOFT limits.
# GEO_GEOAPIFY_API_KEY=
# Override only to point at a proxy or a mock.
# GEO_GEOAPIFY_BASE_URL=https://api.geoapify.com/v1/geocode

# LocationIQ — https://my.locationiq.com (free tier).
# Free tier: 5 000 requests/day but only 2 requests/second, and the limits are
# HARD — there is no soft buffer on the free plan, so going over answers 429
# immediately. That is why it is the chain's reserve rather than its front line.
# GEO_LOCATIONIQ_API_KEY=
# `us1` and `eu1` are separate hosts serving the same API. eu1 is the shorter
# round trip from Cameroon and is the default.
# GEO_LOCATIONIQ_BASE_URL=https://eu1.locationiq.com/v1

# ── Result cache (ADR-A04 D-1) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Redis DB 15. Keyed on the NORMALISED query plus the country bias, the language
# and the requested limit — never the raw string. It FAILS OPEN: a Redis outage
# degrades to direct provider calls, never to a failed address search.
#
# ON by default, unlike most optional infrastructure here, because it is what
# makes the keyless Nominatim default survivable — the public OSM instance
# permits roughly one request per second and bans for abuse.
# GEO_CACHE_ENABLED=true
# How long a RESOLVED result is kept. Addresses do not move.
# GEO_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS=86400
# How long an EMPTY result is kept. Deliberately much shorter: "no match" is the
# answer to a half-typed address AND to a real gap in OpenStreetMap coverage,
# and caching it for a day would hide a newly-mapped street for a day.
# GEO_CACHE_NEGATIVE_TTL_SECONDS=600
# Decimals a reverse-geocode coordinate is rounded to before it becomes a key.
# 4 ≈ 11 m. A GPS fix never repeats exactly, so an unrounded key would have a
# hit rate of zero while looking implemented.
# GEO_CACHE_REVERSE_PRECISION=4
#
# Hit rate: `jovimall_geocoding_cache_events_total{event="hit"|"miss"}` on
# /metrics. ADR-A04 D-2 defers self-hosting until that rate stops rising.

# ── Other providers (config is read; no adapter ships in this build) ─────────
# Google: Cloud Console → APIs & Services → Credentials → Geocoding API key.
# SOURCE: Google Cloud console → APIs & Services → Credentials (Geocoding API).
# GEO_GOOGLE_API_KEY=
# GEO_GOOGLE_BASE_URL=
# Mapbox: account dashboard → Access tokens.
# SOURCE: Mapbox account → Access tokens.
# GEO_MAPBOX_TOKEN=
# GEO_MAPBOX_BASE_URL=


###############################################################################
#  21. BOOKINGS  (`src/modules/booking/`)
###############################################################################

# IANA zone used when a vendor has none set. Wall-clock availability resolves in
# the VENDOR's timezone, never the server's.
# BOOKING_DEFAULT_TIMEZONE=Africa/Douala

# How long a Redis slot hold survives while a customer completes checkout. This
# is the first line of defence only — the transactional CAS at insert is what
# actually guarantees single occupancy, so a Redis restart cannot double-book.
# BOOKING_SLOT_HOLD_TTL_SECONDS=900

# Sweep that cancels confirmed-but-unpaid bookings. Never touches `pending`
# bookings, which are awaiting the vendor rather than the customer.
# BOOKING_UNPAID_EXPIRY_ENABLED=true
# BOOKING_UNPAID_EXPIRY_AFTER_MINUTES=1440   # 24 h
# BOOKING_UNPAID_EXPIRY_INTERVAL_MS=900000   # 15 min
# BOOKING_UNPAID_EXPIRY_BATCH_SIZE=200

# Reminder sweep. Each pass covers [now+lead, now+lead+interval) so consecutive
# passes tile exactly; the idempotency key makes a replay harmless. Changing
# LEAD without regard for INTERVAL can leave a gap no pass covers.
# BOOKING_REMINDER_ENABLED=true
# BOOKING_REMINDER_LEAD_MINUTES=1440         # 24 h before start
# BOOKING_REMINDER_INTERVAL_MS=900000        # 15 min
# BOOKING_REMINDER_BATCH_SIZE=500

# ── Inbound Google Calendar sync ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Keeps the ExternalCalendarBlock cache warm. Availability UNIONS this cache
# with a live query rather than choosing one: subtracting an interval twice is
# idempotent, so a union only ever over-blocks and is self-healing.
# CALENDAR_SYNC_ENABLED=true
# CALENDAR_SYNC_NEAR_INTERVAL_MS=600000      # 10 min
# CALENDAR_SYNC_NEAR_WINDOW_DAYS=30
# CALENDAR_SYNC_FAR_INTERVAL_MS=86400000     # 24 h
# CALENDAR_SYNC_FAR_WINDOW_DAYS=60
# CALENDAR_SYNC_BATCH_SIZE=50                # vendors per batch


###############################################################################
#  22. GOOGLE CALENDAR OAUTH  (`src/modules/integrations/calendar/google/`)
###############################################################################

# Cloud Console → APIs & Services → Credentials → OAuth 2.0 Client ID (Web).
# ⚠ Both have real-looking HARDCODED fallbacks in google-calendar.client.ts, so
# leaving one unset silently authenticates against someone else's OAuth app.
# The boot validator refuses a production start where only one is set.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# SOURCE: Google Cloud console → APIs & Services → Credentials → OAuth 2.0
#         Client IDs. Rotating the secret there invalidates the old one, which
#         breaks the CONSENT flow only — tokens already in the vault keep
#         refreshing.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Must match an Authorized redirect URI on that client, exactly.
# GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:8022/api/integrations/google/callback

# Where the callback sends the browser after connecting. On success it appends
# ?calendar=connected, on failure ?calendar=error&reason=… Unset, the callback
# returns JSON instead of redirecting, which strands the user on a blank page.
# This stays the DEFAULT destination; a caller that names its own returnTo (see
# below) overrides it for that one flow only.
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_FRONTEND_REDIRECT_URL=http://localhost:5173/dashboard/services

# Custom URL schemes a packaged (Capacitor) app may ask the OAuth result to be
# handed back to. Comma-separated, WITHOUT the colon.
#
# A native app has no cookie for /connect to read, and must not load Google's
# consent screen in its own WebView — Google refuses that outright
# (disallowed_useragent). It calls POST /integrations/google/connect-url
# instead, naming a returnTo like `wivendor://services/calendar`, and the OS
# hands that redirect back to the app. The value is allowlisted against this
# variable when the state is minted AND again when it is consumed, and travels
# sealed inside the signed state in between.
#
# Unset, only GOOGLE_OAUTH_FRONTEND_REDIRECT_URL's own origin is accepted: the
# web keeps working and every native connect attempt is refused with a 400,
# which is the correct failure for a deployment that has not configured this.
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_APP_SCHEMES=wivendor

# AES key encrypting the stored OAuth refresh tokens. 64 hex characters:
#     openssl rand -hex 32
# ⚠ Unset, google-token.vault.ts falls back to a literal key committed to this
# repository — every stored token would be encrypted with a public key. The
# boot validator refuses that in production. Rotating this invalidates every
# stored token and every vendor must reconnect.
# SOURCE: generated · openssl rand -hex 32
#         ⚠ NOT ROTATABLE IN PLACE, and it is the only secret here of which
#         that is true. This key ENCRYPTS the stored OAuth refresh tokens
#         (AES-256-GCM, `google-token.vault.ts`), so a new value cannot read
#         what the old one wrote: every connected calendar has to be
#         reconnected by its vendor. GCM authenticates, so the failure is at
#         least loud — decrypt throws rather than returning garbage — but there
#         is no re-key migration, and rotating this is a product decision, not
#         an operational one. ../docs/RUNBOOK.md § Rotation.
# GOOGLE_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY=


###############################################################################
#  23. VECTORISER  (`src/config/vectoriser.config.ts`)
#
#  External product-vectorisation service backing catalogue search: the n8n
#  `wi-mall-vectoriser` workflow, which embeds with Voyage and writes pgvector.
#
#  ⚠ It answers **202** and reports each product's outcome LATER, over
#  `POST /api/internal/vectoriser/callback` — guarded by INTERNAL_SERVICE_TOKEN
#  (§ 8), not by the key below. The key authenticates jovi-mall → n8n; the service
#  token authenticates n8n → jovi-mall. Two directions, two secrets.
###############################################################################

# The n8n PRODUCTION webhook. Must include the `/webhook` segment — the old default
# `https://the8n.fante.cloud/vectoriser` had no such path and posted into a 404.
# VECTORISER_BASE_URL=https://the8n.fante.cloud/webhook/vectorise

# Sent as the `VECTORISER_API_KEY` header. REQUIRED in production; absent in dev the
# requests simply go unauthenticated, which is fine against a local mock.
# SOURCE: the vectoriser deployment (self-hosted — whoever runs it issues it).
# VECTORISER_API_KEY=

# VECTORISER_TIMEOUT_SINGLE_MS=30000
# VECTORISER_TIMEOUT_BULK_MS=120000
# VECTORISER_MAX_RETRIES=3
# Base delay for exponential backoff: attempt 1 waits this, attempt 2 twice it.
# VECTORISER_RETRY_BASE_DELAY_MS=1000


###############################################################################
#  24. BILLING & CREDITS  (`src/modules/billing/config/credit.config.ts`)
#
#  Top-up packs live in that file; per-tier allowances are seeded by
#  `npm run seed:plans`. Only the per-action costs are env-overridable.
###############################################################################

# Credits charged per metered action. 0 makes the action free rather than
# disabled.
# CREDIT_COST_VECTORISATION=5
# CREDIT_COST_WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE=2


###############################################################################
#  25. ORDERS
###############################################################################

# Daily sweep cancelling orders left unpaid past each vendor's window.
# DRY_RUN logs what would be cancelled and mutates nothing.
# UNPAID_ORDER_CANCEL_ENABLED=true
# UNPAID_ORDER_CANCEL_DRY_RUN=false
# UNPAID_ORDER_CANCEL_CRON=0 5 * * *
# UNPAID_ORDER_CANCEL_BATCH_SIZE=200


###############################################################################
#  25b. STOREFRONT — A CUSTOMER'S OWN PRODUCT LISTS & RECOMMENDATIONS
#       (`modules/customers/config/customer-catalog.config.ts`,
#        `modules/catalog/config/related-products.config.ts`)
#
#  Phase 6 · 6.E.1–6.E.3: wishlist, recently viewed, and "customers also bought".
#  All three are storefront conveniences — every value here trades a little
#  accuracy for predictable cost, and none of them is on a money path.
###############################################################################

# How many products the recently-viewed list keeps PER CUSTOMER.
# A CAP, not a page size (that is `?limit`, bounded at 100 like every list here):
# it is how many rows exist at all, enforced on every write by evicting the
# oldest. Nothing prunes by age, so this is also the retention policy for what
# amounts to a record of what a person looked at — say so before raising it.
# CUSTOMER_RECENTLY_VIEWED_CAP=20

# ── Related products / "customers also bought" ───────────────────────────────
#
# The honest signal is co-occurrence in past PAID orders. When a product has
# never been bought alongside anything — most of a young catalogue — the
# endpoint falls back to same-category-recently-ordered and SAYS SO on the wire
# (`meta.source`), because a strip headed "customers also bought" that is really
# ordered by category is a claim about other shoppers that is not true.

# How many products one strip returns.
# RELATED_PRODUCTS_LIMIT=8

# How long a computed ranking is served before recomputing. Hours, not minutes:
# the miss path is an aggregation over `orders`, and the key that expires under
# load is by definition the popular product's. The CARDS are never cached —
# price and stock are read live on every request — so only the ranking ages.
# RELATED_PRODUCTS_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS=21600

# How far back the co-occurrence scan looks. A bound on the QUERY, not a
# judgement about relevance: without it the scan grows with the lifetime of the
# platform and the endpoint gets slower every month unnoticed.
# RELATED_PRODUCTS_WINDOW_DAYS=365

# How many of the subject's recent orders to sample. The ceiling that makes the
# cost independent of how popular the subject is — otherwise the best-seller
# whose page is viewed most is the slowest to answer. It makes the published
# `orders` count a SAMPLE, which api-doc/public/catalog.md states.
# RELATED_PRODUCTS_ORDER_SAMPLE=500

# Below this many co-occurring orders a pairing is dropped as noise. 1 keeps
# every pairing, which is the honest setting on a catalogue with few orders.
# RELATED_PRODUCTS_MIN_CO_OCCURRENCE=1


###############################################################################
#  26. VENDOR SETTINGS
###############################################################################

# Whether a vendor may change their own login email after registration.
# ALLOW_EMAIL_CHANGE=false
# VENDOR_PASSWORD_MIN_LENGTH=8


###############################################################################
#  27. ANALYTICS
###############################################################################

# Vendor analytics aggregation schedule (node-cron).
# ANALYTICS_AGGREGATION_CRON=0 2 * * *


###############################################################################
#  27b. BACKGROUND WORKER OVERLAP LOCK  (`src/core/jobs/worker-lock.ts`)
#
#  Stops a slow sweep overlapping its own next tick, and stops two instances
#  running the same sweep (audit finding F-19). Two layers: an in-process guard
#  that is always on and needs nothing, and a Redis key on WORKER_LOCK_DB (12)
#  that adds the cross-instance half.
###############################################################################

# The Redis layer's off switch. Leave unset in every normal deployment.
#
# Setting it to `false` disables ONLY the cross-instance half — the in-process
# guard stays on, which is the whole guarantee on a single-instance deploy — so
# this is a safe lever if the lock database itself ever becomes the problem.
# `test:system` sets it to drive the guard without a Redis to talk to.
#
# Note the Redis layer already FAILS OPEN on its own: if Redis is unreachable the
# sweep still runs, guarded in-process only. You do not need this to survive an
# outage; it exists for the case where Redis is up and you want the layer off.
# WORKER_LOCK_REDIS=true


###############################################################################
#  28. SYSTEM OPERATIONS  (`src/modules/system/`)
#
#  /api/health/{live,ready}, /metrics, and the read-only diagnostics under
#  /api/internal/admin/system/*. Every value has a production-safe default; the
#  block exists so the knobs are discoverable.
#
#  ⚠ `GET /api/health` is a FROZEN contract — exact path, exact body,
#  unconditional 200. geo-tracker registers it as a READINESS checker and treats
#  any status >= 300 as an error, so putting readiness semantics there means a
#  jovi-mall Redis wobble pulls geo-tracker out of rotation and kills every live
#  WebSocket tracking session. Readiness lives on /api/health/ready instead.
###############################################################################

# Per-dependency probe budget. Each probe runs under Promise.allSettled with
# this timeout, so one hung dependency degrades its own row rather than hanging
# the endpoint.
# HEALTH_PROBE_TIMEOUT_MS=1500

# Whether /api/health/ready requires Redis. DEFAULT FALSE, and that matters:
# Redis connects lazily and never at boot, so a required-Redis probe would
# PROVISION a connection this process never made — on DB 0, which nothing here
# uses — on every probe interval. It would also fail the whole instance for a
# partial capability loss: with Redis down, catalogue, orders, payments and
# shipments all still work. Set true only if you want that coupling on purpose.
# HEALTH_READY_REQUIRE_REDIS=false

# Budget for an on-demand integration probe (GET /system/integrations?probe=…).
# SYSTEM_INTEGRATION_PROBE_TIMEOUT_MS=3000

# How long an instance may serve a cached maintenance verdict before re-reading
# Mongo. This is the cross-instance convergence bound and is reported in the
# mutation response, so an operator knows how long to wait for the fleet to
# agree.
# MAINTENANCE_CACHE_TTL_MS=5000

# Hard deadline on the whole graceful shutdown, after which the process exits
# non-zero regardless. The budget covers every stage: stopping the fourteen
# background workers, draining in-flight requests, waiting out a sweep that
# holds a Redis worker lock, flushing the log sink, and closing Mongo + Redis.
#
# Matches wi-admin's variable of the same name, and sits inside Docker's default
# 10s stop_grace_period — raise BOTH together, or the orchestrator's SIGKILL
# lands first and the larger number here buys nothing.
#
# Sweeps CAN outlast this (earnings release, COD deposit deadlines). That is not
# a failure: the Redis lock's PX expiry is the backstop, and the drain logs which
# keys it left held so the next instance's refused sweep is explainable.
# SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT_MS=10000

# Cache-flush ceilings (POST /dev-tools/cache/flush). Both bound the SCAN loop;
# whichever trips first stops it, and truncation is always reported.
# CACHE_FLUSH_MAX_KEYS=1000
# CACHE_FLUSH_BUDGET_MS=5000

# Ceiling for the read-only key inspector. Lower than the flush's on purpose: a
# listing renders into a dashboard, whereas a flush is clearing a keyspace.
# CACHE_INSPECT_MAX_KEYS=500

# Wall-clock budget for the database inspector. The sweep stops at this bound
# and reports which collections it never reached.
# DB_INSPECT_BUDGET_MS=5000

# ── /metrics exposure ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Enabled by default, for parity with geo-tracker's METRICS_ENABLED.
# METRICS_ENABLED=true

# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION. Unlike geo-tracker this service is internet-facing,
# and an open /metrics gives away per-route-group request volumes (order rate,
# payment rate), the internal route map, every integration and worker name, and
# duration histograms that work as a timing oracle. With NODE_ENV=production and
# this unset, /metrics refuses to serve at all rather than serving openly — so
# Prometheus scrapes 403s until you set it.
#
# Deliberately NOT INTERNAL_ADMIN_SERVICE_TOKEN: a Prometheus scrape config
# lives in a monitoring namespace and is read by more people than a
# full-privilege credential should be.
# SOURCE: generated · openssl rand -hex 32
# METRICS_SCRAPE_TOKEN=

# Comma-separated allowlist, for operators terminating at a sidecar. An
# alternative to the token above, not an addition.
# METRICS_ALLOWED_IPS=

# How long a scraped outbox-depth aggregation may be reused, and its own query
# budget. The aggregation is the expensive part of a scrape.
# METRICS_COLLECT_CACHE_MS=10000
# METRICS_COLLECT_TIMEOUT_MS=2000


###############################################################################
#  29. LOGGING  (`src/core/logging/`)
###############################################################################

# Level for the primary stream.
# LOG_LEVEL=info

# Rewire console.* through the logger. This is what gives the 300+ existing
# `console.error('…', err)` sites structured, searchable stacks. Only the five
# level-shaped methods are swapped.
# ⚠ A test harness must print through `originalConsole` or the bridge swallows
# its own results.
# LOG_CONSOLE_BRIDGE=true

# Whether the human-readable stdout stream is attached. On in every real
# process — stdout is how an operator tails a container.
# LOG_STDOUT=true

# One info line per finished request. Defaults to ON in production only.
# LOG_HTTP_ACCESS=

# Truncation bounds, applied BEFORE scrubbing so a 2 MB payload dump does not
# run seven regexes.
# LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_BYTES=8192
# LOG_MAX_STACK_BYTES=4096

# The in-memory ring buffer behind GET /system/logs. Bounded by both count and
# bytes; whichever fills first evicts.
# LOG_RING_SIZE=2000
# LOG_RING_MAX_BYTES=8388608

# ── Mongo persistence ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Defaults to TRUE IN PRODUCTION ONLY. The justification for persisting is "the
# error survives the restart you go looking for it after", which does not apply
# to `npm run dev` against a shared database.
# LOG_PERSIST_ENABLED=

# Floor is `info` and lower values are CLAMPED, not honoured: persisting debug
# turns the capped collection over in minutes and evicts exactly the errors the
# collection exists to keep.
# LOG_PERSIST_LEVEL=warn

# ⚠ ONE-WAY DOOR. `system_logs` is a CAPPED collection created at first use with
# this size. A capped collection cannot be resized or converted afterwards —
# changing this means dropping the collection, which discards its history.
# LOG_MONGO_CAP_BYTES=268435456     # 256 MB
# LOG_MONGO_CAP_MAX_DOCS=500000

# Sink back-pressure. After FAILURE_STREAK consecutive write failures the sink
# stops trying for COOLDOWN_MS rather than amplifying a database problem into a
# write storm.
# LOG_MONGO_MAX_INFLIGHT=100
# LOG_SINK_FAILURE_STREAK=20
# LOG_SINK_COOLDOWN_MS=60000
# LOG_SINK_ERROR_THROTTLE_MS=60000
# LOG_SINK_FLUSH_BUDGET_MS=1000

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# NEGOTIATION — the bargaining agent's playbook
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# The playbook is the set of instructions served to the bargaining sub-agent as
# its system prefix. It is AUTHORED in git
# (src/modules/negotiation/playbook/negotiation.core.md) and READ from Mongo, so
# the wording can change from a dashboard without a deploy. The bridge between
# the two is `npm run seed:negotiation-playbook`, which runs in one direction
# only and is idempotent by content checksum.
#
# ⚠ Neither variable is a secret and neither has to be set. There is no
# fallback for a MISSING PLAYBOOK, though: the read answers 503
# NEGOTIATION_PLAYBOOK_NOT_PUBLISHED and bargaining is refused. That is
# deliberate - the sub-agent holds the vendor floor in its context and the
# playbook is the whole set of rules about what it may do with that number, so
# "no instructions" has to mean "do not negotiate" rather than "improvise".

# Which playbook the sub-agent is served when a request names none. Must match
# the authored file's frontmatter `name`; test:negotiation-playbook asserts it,
# because a mismatch seeds a row under a key nothing ever reads.
# NEGOTIATION_PLAYBOOK_KEY=market-vendor-negotiation

# How long a resolved playbook is held in process, in seconds. Read on every
# bargaining turn, so this is what keeps a Mongo round-trip off the haggling
# path. In-process rather than Redis - the Redis index budget (5-15) is full,
# and this is a few kilobytes of static text every instance can hold its own
# copy of. The cost is that instances may disagree for up to one TTL after an
# edit, which for a system prompt is a brief prompt-cache miss and nothing else.
# NEGOTIATION_PLAYBOOK_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS=60

# How long a haggle stays resumable. A chat conversation, not a shopping session:
# a customer who comes back an hour later is starting a new negotiation, not
# continuing one. The durable customer profile is what survives.
# NEGOTIATION_SESSION_TTL_MINUTES=30

# How long an agreed price stays spendable.
#
# ⚠ THIS IS THE ENTIRE WINDOW IN WHICH A VENDOR EDIT CAN STRAND A CUSTOMER.
# The lock is re-validated when the order is created and may be REFUSED if the
# vendor has moved the price since (plan D-10) - so this is exactly how long a
# price edit has to break a promise the bot already made. Keep it in minutes.
# NEGOTIATION_LOCK_TTL_MINUTES=20

# The currency a negotiation quotes in. Matches cart.model.ts's own default; the
# variant carries no currency. Both move together when multi-currency lands, or a
# negotiation quotes one and the cart refuses it for a reason nobody can see.
# NEGOTIATION_DEFAULT_CURRENCY=XAF

# The platform's share of the UPLIFT on a haggled line, as a percentage. It funds
# the model spend that produced the uplift.
#
# Read by src/modules/earnings/config/earnings.config.ts, NOT by the negotiation
# module - it is a term of the money split, and the bargaining agent neither knows
# nor needs to know what the platform keeps. It is named NEGOTIATION_* because that
# is where an operator will look for it.
#
# It is a share of (agreedPrice - floor) x quantity, never of the order total. That
# is what guarantees a vendor is never paid below the floor they set themselves, for
# any value in 0..100 - a share of the gross would break that in the first franc.
# Values above 100 are clamped. 0 disables the margin without disabling bargaining.
# NEGOTIATION_AI_MARGIN_PERCENT=30
